Good plan — being organized and having focused questions will make your meeting with Maria Santos much more productive. Below I’ve put together:

- a prioritized list of questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic), with suggested follow-ups you can use verbatim, and
- a short evidence checklist and immediate preservation steps you can take before the next date.

If you’d like, I can also draft an email to Maria Santos that includes these questions and your available times, or turn the checklist into a one‑page packet you can bring to the meeting. Before that, tell me the exact date of the preliminary hearing and the response deadline you were served with so I can tailor priorities.

A. Key questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped and with suggested phrasing)
1) Immediate deadlines and procedural posture
- “What is the single most important deadline I need to meet right now (response to complaint, pretrial filings, discovery deadlines, etc.)?”
  - Follow-up: “What are the consequences if we miss that deadline?”
- “What will happen at the upcoming preliminary hearing and what should I expect to be asked or required to present?”
  - Follow-up: “Will I need to bring any documents or witnesses to that hearing?”
- “Do we need to file any motions before that date (e.g., to extend time, to compel preservation, or for emergency relief)?”

2) Evidence: what’s needed and what’s sufficient
- “Based on the current complaint and our goals, which pieces of evidence are essential to establish my claims/defenses before the next hearing?”
  - Follow-up: “Which items are high priority vs. helpful but optional?”
- “Are there gaps in the evidence you see right now that could hurt our position? If so, what do we need to collect?”
- “Which documents or records should I gather right away (employment contract, pay stubs, performance reviews, relevant emails/texts, termination notices, benefits communications)?”

3) Preservation and collection (do this now)
- “Should we send a preservation/preservation-of-evidence letter to the opposing party or third parties? If so, can you draft and send it?”
- “Are there devices or accounts I should not delete/alter (phones, laptops, cloud accounts, social media, email)?”
  - Follow-up: “Do you recommend making forensic copies or at least backup copies of specific devices?”
- “How should I collect and organize electronic evidence (screenshots vs. native exports, metadata preservation)?”

4) Discovery tools and subpoenas
- “Will we need written discovery (interrogatories, requests for production) or depositions before the hearing?”
  - Follow-up: “What should we ask for in initial discovery to quickly fill gaps?”
- “Do we need to subpoena records from third parties (employer HR files, phone carriers, email providers, banks)? If so, who should issue the subpoenas and when?”
- “What is the usual timeline for getting third‑party records in Maricopa County?”

5) Witnesses and statements
- “Which witnesses do we need to identify and prepare now (coworkers, HR, witnesses to specific events, experts)?”
  - Follow-up: “Should I get signed statements or affidavits from any witnesses right away?”
- “If my ex‑spouse or others gave any prior statements, how do we obtain them and use them?”

6) Expert witnesses and valuation (if applicable)
- “Do we need an expert (forensic accountant, employment expert, psychologist) before the preliminary hearing or later?”
  - Follow-up: “What’s the expected cost and how long to retain one?”

7) Evidence admissibility and chain of custody
- “Are there rules or common objections we should anticipate for the key evidence (authentication of emails, hearsay concerns, business records exception)?”
  - Follow-up: “How do we authenticate electronic communications in this case?”
- “What steps should we take now to preserve chain of custody and make the evidence admissible at trial?”

8) Strategy, settlement posture, and cost
- “Given our facts, what are the realistic outcomes at the hearing and at trial?”
  - Follow-up: “Should we consider settlement talks now, or would that risk weakening our position?”
- “What are the estimated costs and timeline if the case proceeds to trial vs. resolving sooner?”

9) Communications and next steps
- “How often should I expect updates from you, and what is the fastest way to get a quick answer if something urgent comes up?”
- “What do you need from me this week to be ready for the preliminary hearing?”

B. Practical evidence checklist — what to gather now (high priority first)
1) Documents to collect immediately
- Complaint and any summons or papers you were served.
- Employment contract, offer letter, policy manuals, handbook excerpts, performance reviews, disciplinary notices, termination notice(s).
- Any written communications related to the dispute: emails, texts, direct messages, letters, memos.
- Time-stamped evidence (date/time): calendars, meeting invitations, records of in-person meetings.
- Financial records if relevant: pay stubs, bank statements, paycheck stubs, benefits paperwork.
- Any written settlement offers, mediation communications, prior agreements or signed releases.
- Court filings or prior pleadings (if there were earlier filings).

2) Digital and device preservation
- Do NOT delete or alter any emails, text messages, social media posts, or files related to the dispute.
- Make backups (zip or export) of relevant email folders, phone message threads, and files. Save native copies if possible (e.g., .eml or PST for emails).
- Take screenshots with visible timestamps as a backup but also preserve native files which include metadata.
- Note device locations and condition (where your phone/laptop are), and consider putting a hold on any automated deletion settings.

3) Witness and timeline assembly
- Build a chronological timeline of events with dates, times, and short descriptions.
- List witnesses with contact info and what they saw/heard.
- Save any witness contact details and start asking if they’re willing to give a signed statement.

4) Evidence organization
- Create a single folder (physical and digital) labeled with the case name and date range.
- Number and label every document (e.g., EX-001) and keep a master index.
- Keep originals safe; work with copies for sharing.

C. Practical steps to take this week (actions you can do now)
- Confirm and write down the exact hearing date, location, and time. Bring that to your attorney.
- Send Maria a quick message summarizing what you’ve preserved and ask for any immediate instructions.
- Back up any relevant devices and export email/text threads.
- Assemble the timeline and key documents into a single folder and share with Maria (secure upload or in-person).
- If you’re worried opposing party will delete evidence, ask Maria about sending a preservation letter immediately.

D. Suggested short email you can send Maria (I can draft/send if you want)
Subject: Documents & questions ahead of preliminary hearing in [Case Name/Number]
Body (brief):
- “Hi Maria — Our preliminary hearing is on [date]. I’ve attached a folder with the complaint and the documents I’ve collected so far (employment contract, emails, pay stubs, timeline). I’d like to discuss whether we need additional evidence or subpoenas before the hearing. Key questions:
  1) What are the absolute must-have documents before [date]?
  2) Do we need preservation letters/subpoenas now?
  3) Can we schedule a short call this week to review next steps?
Thanks — [Your name]”

E. How I can help next
- Draft the exact email to Maria Santos and, if you want, send it (I can draft and leave it for you, or send if you give the OK and provide her address).
- Convert the evidence checklist into a one‑page packet you can print and bring to the meeting.
- Create a timeline template you can fill in and send to your attorney.
- Help you prioritize documents if you tell me the hearing date and what you already have collected.

If you share:
- the preliminary hearing date,
- the response deadline you were served with,
