Good—this is the right time to get organized. Below I’ve put together (1) a prioritized list of questions to ask Maria Santos at your next contact, (2) a concrete checklist of documents/evidence to gather and preserve now, (3) immediate actions to take before the preliminary hearing/response deadline, and (4) a short email template you can send to Maria asking what she needs. If you want, I can turn your answers into an organized exhibit list or draft declarations/requests for preservation.

1) Priority questions to ask your attorney (ordered by urgency)
- Deadlines & filings
  - What is the exact deadline to file my formal response, answer, or motion? Can we extend it safely and how?
  - What filings will you make before the preliminary hearing (response, motion to dismiss, motion to continue, request for extension, preservation letter)?
  - Are there any local Maricopa County or Arizona rules we must follow for this stage?

- Evidence & preservation
  - Based on the complaint/claims, what specific evidence do you need me to secure right away?
  - Do you want me to send originals, copies, or both? How should I label and submit them?
  - Should we send a formal litigation hold or preservation notice to the opposing party or third parties (employer, IT, banks)?
  - Are there specific items where you recommend forensic capture (emails, devices, servers)?

- Discovery plan & scope
  - What discovery will we pursue first (document requests, interrogatories, requests for admission, subpoenas, depositions)?
  - What evidence do you anticipate the other side will request, and what do we need to prepare to respond?
  - Are there potential third parties we should subpoena (former colleagues, HR, banks)?

- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses should I contact now for written statements or to confirm availability (colleagues, supervisors, witnesses to relevant events)?
  - Do we need signed, sworn declarations or affidavits before the hearing?

- Experts & technical evidence
  - Do you think we need an expert (employment expert, damages expert, IT/forensic)?
  - If yes, what is the timeline and cost estimate to retain one?

- Authentication & chain of custody
  - What will the court require to authenticate documents/electronic evidence? Do you want originals, certified copies, or printouts with metadata?
  - Should I ask custodians to preserve metadata and not delete anything?

- Strategy & settlement
  - What are our legal theories and likely defenses/claims?
  - Should we try to negotiate a short extension or a settlement to avoid trial? If settlement is an option, what information do you need to evaluate offers?

- Logistics & costs
  - What is the estimated cost/time for discovery and trial preparation if this goes to trial?
  - What deadlines/steps should I calendar and who will manage filings/serving?

2) Documents and evidence to gather and preserve now
- Employment & contract documents
  - Original employment contract(s), offer letters, job description, amendments, severance agreements.
  - Any non-compete, confidentiality, or arbitration agreements.

- Payroll & financial records
  - Pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, bank statements, payroll summaries, benefits statements, bonus/commission records.

- Termination-related documents
  - Termination letter, HR emails, performance reviews, disciplinary records, warnings, exit paperwork.

- Communications
  - All emails, text messages, direct messages, chat logs (Slack/Teams) related to the dispute, from you and the opposing party.
  - Save entire message threads with timestamps and sender/recipient headers.
  - Social media posts/messages relevant to the case.

- Contracts, invoices, and contemporaneous documents
  - Any contracts, amendments, invoices, receipts, or calendars that show dates and obligations.

- Witness info & statements
  - Names, contact info, short notes about what each witness saw/heard, and any available written statements.

- Evidence of damages
  - Receipts, invoices, proof of lost income, medical bills (if relevant), or other quantifiable losses.

- Records showing reason for dispute (if relevant)
  - Performance metrics, emails about disputes, HR investigation notes, complaints.

- Electronic evidence preservation
  - Export/save emails as PDFs including full headers; take screenshots with visible timestamps; back up phones and devices.
  - Preserve original devices and do not factory-reset or delete relevant messages.
  - If needed, create forensic images (ask counsel first).

3) How to preserve and organize materials (practical steps)
- Immediately copy all relevant electronic data to a secure location (external hard drive or encrypted cloud). Do not delete originals.
- For emails: Export full message with headers (not just body). If uncertain how, save as PDF and include the “View source” or header info.
- For texts: Use a tool or your carrier’s backup to capture full threads with timestamps. Take screenshots as interim proof.
- Label files with date, source, and short description (e.g., 2025-02-11_Email_HR_Termination.pdf).
- Create a single index/spreadsheet listing each item, date, sender, short description, and where the file is stored.
- If someone witnessed events, get short signed statements or contemporaneous notes and the witness’s best contact info.
- Preserve originals when possible and provide counsel with both originals and copies.

4) Immediate actions to take (priority checklist for the next 48–72 hours)
- Confirm the exact response deadline and calendar it with reminders.
- Send/ask me to help send a short note to Maria summarizing what you’ve already collected and ask what she needs right away.
