Good — that’s a lot to manage on a tight timeline. Below I’ll give (A) focused questions to ask Maria Santos, (B) an immediate checklist of things you should do now, (C) the documents/witnesses you should try to gather or preserve, (D) how to prioritize evidence and typical timing, and (E) short sample phrasing you can use with your attorney (including a preservation note and a request for an extension). If you’d like, I can draft an email to Maria with these questions and your facts — tell me the hearing date and any evidence you already have.

A. Focused questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & deadlines
  - What are the exact upcoming deadlines (preliminary hearing date, deadline to file a response, discovery cutoffs, motion deadlines)?
  - Do we have time to obtain additional evidence before the preliminary hearing? If not, will it matter for this hearing vs. trial?
  - Should we ask the court for a continuance or extension of time to gather evidence?

- Evidence & what’s needed
  - What specific evidence would be most helpful to support my contract-dispute claims/defenses (contracts, communications, payroll, performance reviews, witness testimony, etc.)?
  - Which items are essential to have before the next date versus things we can develop later?
  - Are there likely authenticity or hearsay issues with the evidence I possess (emails, texts, HR notes)? How can we authenticate them?

- Discovery & collection
  - What discovery tools should we use right now (document requests, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas for records, depositions of witnesses)?
  - Can we issue subpoenas (e.g., for employer records, bank records, phone provider) and how long do those typically take to produce?
  - Do we need written statements or sworn affidavits from potential witnesses now? Should witnesses prepare a timeline or notes?

- Preservation & spoliation
  - Should we send a preservation/preservation-of-evidence letter to the opposing party and any third parties (HR, IT, banks)?
  - What steps should I take to preserve electronic evidence (emails, phones, laptops, cloud storage, social media)?
  - If evidence is destroyed, what remedies are available and how do we prove spoliation?

- Strategy & next steps
  - What is the plan for the preliminary hearing — is it evidentiary or procedural in Maricopa County for this matter? What should I expect (testimony, exhibits, scheduling)?
  - If the case will likely proceed to trial, when should we start lining up experts, witnesses, and exhibits?
  - What are the chances of settlement vs going to trial and what evidence would increase settlement leverage?

- Costs & timing
  - What are the estimated costs and timeframes for obtaining each type of evidence (forensic exam, subpoenas, depositions, experts)?
  - Can we limit costs by prioritizing certain evidence?

- Confidentiality & sensitive info
  - Should we seek a protective order (if records contain sensitive personal or proprietary info)?
  - Are there communications I must avoid (social media, talking to the opposing party) to avoid hurting our case?

B. Immediate checklist (what to do today / within 48 hours)
- Preserve everything: Do not delete messages, documents, or files. Make copies/backups of:
  - Emails, text messages, direct messages, and call logs
  - Employment records: contract(s), offer letters, termination letters, pay stubs, benefits documents
  - Performance reviews, HR complaints, internal memos
  - Any written agreements, NDAs, or relevant attachments
  - Calendar entries and meeting notes
- Create a simple chronological timeline of events (dates, people, documents) — even a one-page timeline helps your lawyer quickly evaluate gaps.
- Write a short summary for each potential witness (name, contact, what they saw/knew, how to reach them).
- Save metadata where possible (export emails as files/PDFs, screenshot with timestamps, preserve originals).
- Avoid discussing the case publicly or with the opposing party (let your lawyer handle contact).
- Ask your attorney to send a preservation letter to the opposing counsel and third parties now.

C. Documents & evidence to gather (high priority first)
- Contracts and written terms of employment, amendments, and related agreements
- Emails and texts with the employer/ex-spouse about job duties, termination, compensation, and dispute
- Offer letters, termination notices, HR investigation reports, policy manuals
- Payroll records, pay stubs, expense reimbursements, benefits statements
- Performance reviews, disciplinary records, internal complaints
- Records of communications with third parties that corroborate your claims (vendors, co-workers)
- Calendar entries, meeting invitations, recorded Zoom/Teams logs if applicable
- Bank statements or payment records that show contract performance or damages
- Social media posts relevant to timeline (capture screenshots and metadata)
- Any signed releases, settlements, or prior agreements
- Witness contact info and short written recollections (date and signature if possible)

D. Prioritization and timing (rough estimates)
- Preservation letter: immediate (within days)
- Collecting your own documents and witness statements: 1–7 days (you can do this quickly)
- Subpoenas for employer records or third-party records: typically 2–4 weeks or more (service + production time)
- Depositions: schedule-dependent; can take a few weeks to set up
- Expert retention (if needed): 2–6+ weeks depending on specialty
- If the preliminary hearing is very soon, focus on preservation, your essential documents, and witness availability. Ask if the hearing is primarily procedural (scheduling/initial matters) or evidentiary.

E. Suggested phrasing to use with your attorney (quick templates)
- Request for preservation letter:
  “Maria — please send a preservation letter to opposing counsel and any relevant third parties (HR, IT, payroll, banks) instructing them to preserve all documents, emails, and electronic data related to [case name/parties]. I have preserved my copies but want formal preservation in place now.”
- Request for continuance/extension:
  “Maria — given the short window and pending discovery needs, do you recommend asking the court for a continuance so we can obtain subpoenas and witness statements? If so, can you file a motion or stipulation to extend the response/hearing deadlines?”
- Asking whether to subpoena records:
  “Which records should we subpoena immediately (HR files, payroll, phone records, cloud backups)? How long will each subpoena take and what are associated costs?”
- Asking about evidence authenticity:
  “Do we need to have emails/texts forensically preserved or authenticated? Will business-records affidavits be sufficient for employer documents?”

F. How I can help next
- I can draft the email(s) to Maria asking the above questions and proposing a plan.
- I can draft a short preservation letter template for your attorney to send to opposing counsel (you can customize with names/dates).
- I can make a checklist template or one-page timeline for you to fill in and then format into a document to give Maria.

If you want me to draft an email to Maria Santos now, tell me:
1) The date of the preliminary hearing and any other deadlines you know,
2) Which evidence/documents you already have,
3) Whether you want to ask for an extension/continuance now.

