Good — I can help you prepare. Below is a prioritized plan plus focused questions and a document checklist you can take to your meeting or call with Maria Santos. These are practical, attorney-directed questions to determine whether you need more evidence before the next date and what immediate steps to take.

Immediate priorities (what to do right now)
- Confirm the exact deadline(s) and next court date with Maria (filing deadlines, service deadlines, hearing date). Ask her to put critical dates on your calendar.
- Preserve all potential evidence now (see preservation actions below).
- Gather and organize the most important documents (checklist below) so Maria can quickly review whether anything critical is missing.
- Ask whether you should request a short continuance if more evidence collection is needed and time is tight.

Questions to ask Maria (grouped and phrased for a meeting or email)
1) Case status, deadlines, and procedure
- What is the exact date and purpose of the next court appearance? What must be done before then?
- What are the critical filing deadlines I must meet (answer/response, motions, discovery responses)?
- Is the matter in civil division in Maricopa County governed by any local rules or special procedures I should know right away?

2) Evidence sufficiency and priorities
- Based on what I’ve already provided, do we have enough evidence to meet our objectives at the next hearing?
- Which pieces of evidence (documents, witnesses, emails, HR records, etc.) are essential vs. “nice to have”?
- What are the biggest factual gaps that could hurt our position at the preliminary hearing or in later discovery?

3) Preservation and spoliation
- What immediate steps should I take to preserve evidence (emails, devices, paper files, social media, company files)?
- Should we send a written preservation/hold letter to the former employer or any third parties? If so, can you send it on our letterhead?
- Do we need to take any emergency steps to prevent spoliation (e.g., forensics of devices)?

4) Discovery — how and when to get more evidence
- What discovery tools should we use now (document requests, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas to third parties)?
- Can we get key employer records (HR files, payroll, email, CCTV) voluntarily or will we need subpoenas? How long does that take in Maricopa County?
- Should we or can we subpoena communications between the opposing party and any third parties (co-workers, HR, witnesses)?

5) Witnesses and statements
- Which witnesses should we plan to call or depose before the hearing/trial?
- Should we obtain written witness statements/affidavits now? Would you prefer sworn affidavits or signed declarations?
- How should we approach witnesses so as not to jeopardize their testimony or create problems (e.g., coaching)?

6) Experts and technical evidence
- Do we likely need an expert for any issues (forensic accountant, labor/contract expert, IT forensics)? If so, when do we need to retain them?
- What documentation would an expert need to evaluate our case?

7) Admissibility and foundation
- Are there potential authentication or hearsay issues with the documents I have (emails, text messages, screenshots)?
- How should we authenticate electronic evidence (metadata, originals, chain of custody)?

8) Strategy and settlement
- What is our immediate litigation strategy for the next hearing (ask for continuance, focus on jurisdiction, evidentiary showing, preliminary relief)?
- Do you recommend exploring settlement or mediation now? If so, what evidence would strengthen our settlement posture?
- What are the risks/costs of waiting to gather additional evidence vs. proceeding on the current timeline?

9) Logistics, costs, and timeline
- How long will it take you to review the materials I bring and advise what’s missing?
- Rough cost/time estimates for obtaining third-party records, depositions, and expert reports.
- If we need an extension, what is the likelihood the court will grant a continuance?

10) Communications and confidentiality
- What can I say or not say publicly or on social media while the matter is pending?
- How should I handle any contact or attempts to communicate from the opposing party (my ex-spouse)? Should I refer them to you?

Document & evidence checklist to gather now (prioritize the top items)
Top-priority documents (get these first)
- The written contract(s) at issue (signed copies, amendments, related agreements)
- Termination, severance, or related employer notices
- Letters, emails, texts, Slack messages, or other communications between you and the employer about the dispute
- Any written communications with your ex-spouse related to the disputed contract or issues in the case
- Notices of service, summons, complaint, and any court papers you’ve received
- Pay stubs, payroll records, benefits records, expense reports, invoices, and billing records relevant to the contract
- Performance reviews, disciplinary records, HR investigations, emails from HR
- Any receipts, invoices, bills, or financial documents tied to the dispute
- Witness names, contact information, and brief descriptions of what they saw/know

Secondary documents (collect if applicable)
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, internal memos
- Recordings, voicemails, CCTV where legally obtained
- Screenshots of messages (also preserve originals and metadata)
- Social media posts related to the dispute
- Employment handbook, policies, non-compete or NDA documents
- Any prior settlement offers or communications about settlement

How to organize and present materials to Maria
- Create a concise chronology/timeline (one page) of key events with dates.
- Label and index documents (e.g., Doc1 – Contract signed 01/15/2024). Put originals in a secure folder and create scanned PDF copies.
- Create an electronic folder with subfolders (Court filings, Contract, Communications, HR, Financials, Witnesses). Back it up.
- Provide a short summary (1–2 sentences) next to each document explaining why it’s relevant.

Preservation steps you can take immediately
- Save and back up emails and attachments (export mailboxes or print to PDF).
- Stop deleting related messages, texts, or documents. Preserve devices.
- Take screenshots if needed, but also preserve original files with metadata.
- Save social media posts and relevant profiles as PDFs.
- Tell anyone who might have relevant documents (witnesses, co-workers) to retain them and not delete anything.

Questions about specific tactics to ask Maria
- Should I request a protective order or confidentiality agreement for sensitive documents?
- Should we file any preliminary motions (e.g., to exclude evidence, for temporary injunctive relief, or to compel preservation)?
- If time is extremely tight, which single action (document or witness) would you prioritize to strengthen our position?

Suggested meeting agenda (15–30 minute call)
1. Confirm upcoming court dates and exact deadlines (5 min)
2. Provide timeline and top 5 documents you’ve gathered (5–10 min)
3. Ask the priority questions about evidence and discovery (5–10 min)
4. Agree on next steps and who will do what (preservation, subpoenas, expert retention) (5 min)

Offer to help
- I can draft a short email or meeting agenda to Maria with the specific questions above.
- I can help you make the one-page timeline or an indexed list of documents to hand to her.
- If you want, tell me which documents you already have and I’ll highlight what’s missing and suggest which items to get first.

Note: this is guidance to help you prepare questions for your attorney. For legal advice about strategy, deadlines, or court procedure, rely on Maria Santos as your counsel.

