--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 7627 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized. Below is a focused, practical checklist you can use when you speak with Maria Santos, plus a prioritized evidence collection plan, preservation steps, and a short template email you can send her. If you want, I can draft the actual email to Maria or turn the checklist into a one-page packet you can print or attach.

Quick clarifying questions before we go further
- What is the exact next court date (preliminary hearing date)?
- Do you already have the complaint/summons and a deadline to file a response? If so, what’s the response deadline?
- Do you have an attorney-client relationship already in place with Maria (retainer signed) or are you just consulting?
- What type of contract dispute (employment contract, severance, commission, breach of noncompete, other)?
Answering these will let me tailor the list.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case status & deadlines
  - What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing? What issues will the judge decide at this stage?
  - What are all upcoming deadlines I must meet (response to complaint, discovery cutoff, motions, pretrial)?
  - Should we ask for a continuance or more time to prepare?
- Evidence collection & preservation
  - Based on the complaint, what specific evidence do you think we need before the next court date?
  - Which documents/electronic records are highest priority to collect now?
  - Should we send a preservation/preservation-of-evidence letter to the opposing party or third parties?
- Discovery & subpoenas
  - Will we need to issue discovery requests or subpoenas before the hearing/trial? Can any discovery be expedited?
  - If third-party records (bank, phone, email provider, HR) are needed, how and when will we obtain them?
- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses should we interview or get written declarations from now?
  - Do we need to preserve witness contact info and availability for the hearing?
- Strategy & outcomes
  - What is our immediate strategy for the preliminary hearing (dismissal, settlement, preserve rights, emergency relief)?
  - What evidence would most strengthen our position at the hearing vs. at trial?
- Practical matters
  - What are the estimated costs and timelines for obtaining more evidence (forensics, subpoenas, expert reports)?
  - How should I deliver/organize evidence for you (email, secure portal, physical copies)?
  - Is there anything I should avoid doing (e.g., deleting emails, contacting certain people)?
- Settlement/ADR
  - Are there settlement or mediation options before or after the preliminary hearing? Should we prepare settlement authority or bottom-line positions?
- Next steps
  - What should I do in the next 48–72 hours? What should I do if opposing counsel contacts me?

Priority evidence checklist — what to collect right now
(Collect originals if possible; scan & back up immediately)
1. Core contract materials
   - Original contract(s), amendments, signed pages, addenda, exhibits, and any contemporaneous drafts showing negotiations or changes.
2. Communications showing formation/performance/breach
   - Emails, text messages, instant messages, LinkedIn messages, letters, and any voice mail transcripts between you and the employer/ex-spouse or their agents about the contract terms, performance, termination, payments, or disputes.
3. Payment/financial records
   - Pay stubs, invoices, bank deposits, cancelled checks, expense reports, commission statements, payroll records showing amounts owed/paid.
4. HR and employment records (if applicable)
   - Offer letters, termination notices, performance reviews, disciplinary records, benefits/severance documents.
5. Documents showing damages or mitigation
   - Bills, lost-income calculations, receipts, proof of mitigation efforts (applications, search records).
6. Third-party documents
   - Contracts or communications with clients, vendors, or colleagues that relate to the dispute.
7. Timeline/notes
   - Chronological timeline of key events in short bullet form (dates, one-line descriptions), meeting notes, contemporaneous journals.
8. Witness list & contact info
   - Names, phone/email, relation, short summary of what each person can testify to.
9. Electronic evidence & metadata
   - Original electronic files, emails with headers, photos with timestamps, document version history.
10. Anything you were served with or sent
   - Complaint, summons, any correspondence from opposing counsel or process server.

How to preserve and organize evidence (immediately)
- Do not delete anything. Stop any deletion or purging of emails, texts, or files related to the dispute.
- Save originals and make backups: scan physical docs to PDF; export emails (with headers) and text threads. Make at least two backups (secure cloud + external drive).
- Maintain a simple folder structure (e.g., 01_Contract, 02_Communications, 03_Payments, 04_Witnesses). Use consistent file naming: YYYYMMDD_sender_subject.pdf.
- Create an Evidence Index (spreadsheet) listing file name, brief description, date, and location (hard copy/drive).
- Preserve metadata for electronic files. If unsure, ask your attorney about forensic preservation.
- If relevant evidence is held by a third party (bank, employer, phone carrier), ask Maria about sending preservation letters immediately and subpoena options.
- Take dated screenshots of web pages or social media; note when you captured them.

Preparing witnesses
- Ask key witnesses for a short written memo of what they recall (date, facts, contact info).
- Make sure witnesses know dates/times they may be needed and do not coach or alter recollections.
- Provide witness contact list to your attorney promptly.

Short-term priorities (if you have a short window)
1. Confirm hearing date and response deadline with Maria.
2. Produce the top-priority documents to Maria (contract, complaint, key emails, timeline).
3. Ask Maria whether to send preservation letters or immediate discovery.
4. Get witness contact info to Maria today/tomorrow.
5. Decide whether to ask the court for more time if evidence collection cannot be done in time.

Sample short email you can send to Maria Santos
(You can copy/paste & edit; I can draft/send it if you want.)
