I can help you get organized and make sure you ask Maria the right questions before the preliminary hearing. I’m not a lawyer, so treat this as organizational guidance to use in your conversation with Maria Santos at Santos Law Office.

Below are (A) focused questions to ask your attorney, (B) immediate preservation steps you can take, (C) a prioritized evidence-collection checklist, (D) what to bring to your meeting, and (E) a short sample email you can send to Maria asking for an urgent call/meeting and listing the key items.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & deadlines
  - What are the upcoming deadlines (response to complaint, preliminary hearing date, filing deadlines)? Are any of them firm?
  - Can we get an extension if we need time to collect evidence? What’s the process and likelihood?
  - What should be filed or requested before the preliminary hearing?

- Evidence and discovery
  - Based on everything you know now, is the evidence we have sufficient to meet our goals at the preliminary hearing (or to avoid a default or adverse ruling)?
  - What specific pieces of evidence should I prioritize collecting before the next date?
  - Should we send a preservation/preservation-of-evidence letter to the opposing party or third parties?
  - Do we need formal discovery (interrogatories, document requests, subpoenas) now — or can we wait until after the preliminary hearing?
  - If relevant records are held by third parties (former employer, banks, cloud providers, telecoms), can we issue subpoenas now? How long will that take?

- Admissibility, proof & witnesses
  - Which documents or communications are likely to be admissible or persuasive (contracts, emails, termination letter, performance reviews, payroll records)?
  - Are there witness statements we should get now (co-workers, supervisors, HR, third-party witnesses)? Should those be sworn declarations or informal notes?
  - Will we need an expert (forensic accountant, employment expert) for trial or even to support a preliminary motion?

- Urgent motions & protective steps
  - Should we file any immediate motions (for a stay, protective order, emergency relief, preservation order)?
  - Do we need a gag order or confidentiality steps given the ex-spouse involvement?
  - If opposing counsel is doing discovery or harassing you, what immediate steps do we take?

- Strategy & risks
  - What’s our strategy for the preliminary hearing (settle, narrow issues, get discovery permission)?
  - What risks do we face if we don’t produce additional evidence now?
  - What’s the likely timeline to trial and what additional evidence will be critical for trial preparation?

- Logistics & costs
  - What are estimated costs for collecting evidence (forensics, subpoenas, expert reports)?
  - How should I deliver documents to you and how much time will you need to review?

- Communication & confidentiality
  - How should I communicate new evidence to you (secure portal, email, in person)?
  - Are there any communications I must avoid with the opposing party or on social media?

