Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — this is the right time to prepare. Below I’ve put together a focused, practical checklist of what to ask Maria Santos and concrete steps you can take now to preserve and collect evidence before the preliminary hearing. I’ve grouped questions by topic and included sample wording you can read or send to your attorney. If you want, I can also draft an email or a preservation letter you can send to third parties or to your lawyer.

Immediate priorities (do these first)
- Preserve everything now: do not delete emails, texts, voicemails, social media posts, calendars, documents, paystubs, contracts, HR records, or files on your devices. Make backups (export/save PDFs, screenshots with timestamps).
- Create a simple timeline of events (dates, short description, who was involved). Even a one-page chronology helps your lawyer quickly spot gaps.
- Gather original documents you already have: signed contract(s), termination/severance letters, pay records, performance reviews, any correspondence with employer or ex-spouse about the contract, and any service papers.
- Note potential witnesses (names, contact details, what they can say).
- Make a short list of things you think are missing or that could support your version of events.

Questions to ask Maria (organized and ready-to-use)
1) Case status, deadlines, and immediate strategy
- “What exactly will happen at the preliminary hearing and what decisions can be made there?”
- “What are the filing deadlines I must meet before the hearing (responses, disclosures, exhibits)?”
- “Do we need more time to gather evidence? If so, should we file a motion for continuance now?”
- “Are there any upcoming deadlines under local Maricopa County civil rules I should know about?”

2) What evidence we need and why
- “Based on our current claims/defenses, what specific evidence is most important for the preliminary hearing and what is needed later at trial?”
- “Which documents or communications are essential to prove breach (or your defense) and damages?”
- “Are there particular standards of proof or types of evidence the court in Maricopa County tends to require for this kind of contract dispute?”

3) Preservation, collection, and authentication
- “What immediate preservation steps should I take for electronic evidence (email, texts, social media, phone backups)?”
- “Should we send a preservation/spoliation letter to the opposing party or third parties (employer, IT, cloud providers)?”
- “How do we authenticate documents or digital records (metadata, affidavits, witness testimony)?”

4) Discovery and third-party evidence
- “What discovery will we pursue before the next date (written discovery, subpoenas, document requests, depositions)?”
- “Can we subpoena employer records, payroll, HR files, or communications from third parties? If yes, how quickly can we do that?”
- “Do we need subpoenas duces tecum for phone providers, email hosts, or social platforms?”

5) Witnesses and testimony
- “Which witnesses should we prepare for the preliminary hearing and which are candidates for deposition or trial?”
- “How should we prepare witnesses (statements, practice questions, what to avoid)?”
- “Are there witnesses we should subpoena now because they may become unavailable?”

6) Expert evidence
- “Do we need an expert (e.g., damages expert, contract interpretation, forensic IT) before the next hearing or only if the case goes to trial?”
- “If we need an expert, how long will it take and what is the cost estimate?”

7) Admissibility and legal hurdles
- “Are there likely hearsay, privilege, or authentication issues with the documents we plan to use?”
- “How do we handle communications involving the opposing party who is also my ex-spouse — does family law procedure affect evidence or confidentiality here?”

8) Motions and defensive options
- “Should we consider filing any motions before the hearing (motion to dismiss, motion to compel discovery, motion for protective order, motion for continuance)?”
- “If they ask for injunctive relief or other urgent remedies, how should we respond?”

9) Settlement and negotiation posture
- “Is settlement appropriate at this stage or would a premature settlement be risky? What information would you need to evaluate any offer?”
- “If settlement talks start, what documents or evidence should we prepare to support our negotiating position?”

10) Costs, timeline, and communication
- “What are the likely costs/time needed to gather the evidence you recommend?”
- “How will you update me and how much lead time do you need to get me prepped for the hearing?”

Concrete evidence checklist (what to gather now)
- Signed contract(s), amendments, scope-of-work, SOWs, and related exhibits
- Employment-related: offer letters, termination letters, severance agreements, payroll records, tax forms (W-2/1099), performance reviews
- All communications: emails, text messages, WhatsApp, Slack, direct messages, letters (save in original format and as PDFs/screenshots)
- HR files and communications with employer
- Calendar entries/meeting invitations showing relevant dates
- Invoices, payment records, bank statements showing damages or payments owed
- Witness names and short statement of what they can confirm
- Any recordings, voicemails, or meeting notes
- Social media posts or public statements relevant to the dispute
- Forensic evidence: metadata, file properties, server logs if applicable

How to prioritize if time is short
1. Preserve: don’t delete anything (immediately). Make copies/backups.
2. Collect the critical documents listed above — contract, termination/severance, emails that prove the key facts.
3. Prepare the one-page timeline and witness list.
4. Ask Maria whether a preservation letter or immediate subpoena is necessary for employer or third-party records.
5. Ask your attorney if they recommend a motion for continuance if you can’t collect essential evidence before the hearing.

Sample wording you can use when you call or email Maria
- “Maria — I have a preliminary hearing on [date]. I want to be sure we have the evidence needed. Can we review what specific documents and witnesses you need from me before the hearing and whether we should send a preservation letter or seek subpoenas now?”
- “Do we need to ask the court for a continuance to obtain third-party records? If so, what basis would support a continuance?”
- “Please advise which of the attached documents you want me to prioritize collecting and whether you need me to prepare witness contact info or statements.”

Other practical tips
- Keep a single folder (physical or digital) labeled with the case name and date for all documents you collect.
- Use consistent filenames and note when/where each file came from (source + date).
- If any evidence is on someone else’s device/account (employer, cloud), tell Maria now so she can subpoena it if necessary.
- Ask Maria about privilege issues — be careful when sharing privileged communications with third parties.

Next steps I can help with
- Draft an email to Maria summarizing the above and asking for specific instructions (I can tailor it with your hearing date).
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in.
- Draft a preservation/spoliation letter to send to the employer or third parties (you should run it by Maria first).

If you want me to draft an email to Maria Santos now, tell me:
- The date of the preliminary hearing
- Anything you’ve already collected (list of key documents)
- Whether you want me to include an ask for a continuance or subpoenas

Would you like me to draft that email or a preservation letter now?

