Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I can help you get organized and ready for the next steps. Below are focused questions you can ask Maria Santos plus a prioritized checklist of evidence and immediate actions so you can decide what to collect before the preliminary hearing. I also include a short sample email you can send to Maria if you’d like.

Important: I’m not your lawyer. These are practical, preparatory suggestions — confirm all deadlines and legal strategy with Maria Santos (Santos Law Office).

Key questions to ask your attorney (with short sample phrasing and why it matters)
- Deadlines & immediate filing needs
  - Sample: “What is the exact deadline to respond or file an answer/motion in Maricopa County, and what happens if we miss it?”
  - Why: You need to know hard timelines and whether extensions or emergency filings are possible.

- Scope and purpose of the preliminary hearing
  - Sample: “What exactly will the judge decide at the preliminary hearing (procedural scheduling, temporary relief, evidentiary rulings)? What should we be prepared to present?”
  - Why: That determines how much evidence or witnesses you must have ready.

- Evidence preservation / spoliation
  - Sample: “What immediate steps should I take to preserve documents and electronic evidence? Should I be under a preservation notice, and do I need to stop deleting files/records?”
  - Why: Preserving evidence (emails, devices, cloud accounts) is critical and time sensitive.

- Which documents/evidence matter most now
  - Sample: “Which specific documents/electronic records are highest priority for the preliminary hearing and for trial (employment contract, communications, payroll, etc.)?”
  - Why: Prioritize collection so you don’t waste time on low-value items.

- Witnesses and statements
  - Sample: “Who should we identify as witnesses now? Should I/you get written declarations or recorded statements before the hearing?”
  - Why: Early written statements lock in testimony and reduce risk of losing witnesses.

- Discovery plan and timing
  - Sample: “What discovery should we seek immediately (doc requests, interrogatories, preservation letters, subpoenas)? When should we serve them?”
  - Why: Early discovery can compel key documents and preserve evidence from third parties (banks, IT providers, HR).

- Experts and valuations
  - Sample: “Do we need an expert (forensic accountant, HR expert, IT forensic) at this stage? If so, when should we retain one?”
  - Why: Experts take time to prepare reports and may be needed to quantify damages or recover deleted electronic evidence.

- Confidentiality / protective orders
  - Sample: “Should we ask the court for a protective order or confidentiality agreement if we must disclose sensitive documents?”
  - Why: Protects personal or proprietary information during discovery.

- Settlement posture / negotiations
  - Sample: “Is settlement likely or recommended before the hearing? What would be our minimum acceptable outcome?”
  - Why: Knowing settlement strategy affects how much to invest in evidence now.

- Communications and public statements
  - Sample: “Are there restrictions on what I should say (to the opposing party, witnesses, or on social media)?”
  - Why: Avoiding damaging statements preserves credibility and avoids creating more evidence for the other side.

Prioritized evidence checklist — what to gather now
(Collect and preserve originals where possible; make time-stamped copies/backups.)

1. Contract & employment-related documents
  - Employment agreement, amendments, offer letters, non-compete, NDAs, severance agreements, termination letter.
  - Job descriptions, performance reviews, disciplinary records.

2. Compensation & financial records
  - Pay stubs, W-2s/1099s, bonus/commission records, bank statements, expense reimbursements, benefits records.

3. Communications
  - Emails, text messages, Slack/Teams messages, direct messages, social media messages with employer, ex-spouse, HR, or relevant coworkers.
  - Save message metadata (timestamps); export rather than screenshot if possible.

4. HR files & corporate documents
  - HR complaint forms, investigation reports, meeting notes, internal policies, personnel file entries.

5. Evidence of performance or damages
  - Client lists (if relevant), lost earnings calculations, invoices, proof of mitigation (applications, other job offers), receipts for expenses related to dispute.

6. Witness info
  - Names, contact details, short notes on what they observed, and dates/times of relevant events.

7. Electronic evidence & logs
  - Device backups (phone, laptop), cloud storage contents, file version history, access logs, IP logs if relevant.
  - Preserve devices — do not factory reset or delete content.

8. Service & court papers
  - Copies of the complaint, summons, proof of service, any motions or orders already filed.

9. Third-party records
  - Bank records, payroll vendor records, HR vendor communications, emails from other entities. These may require subpoenas.

10. Timeline/chronology
  - Create a one-page chronology of key events with dates — easiest for your attorney to review quickly.

Immediate actions (first 48–72 hours)
- Preserve everything: stop deleting emails, texts, or files; make backups; keep devices powered.
- Save copies of cloud data (download emails, export messages).
- Compile a chronological summary (1–2 pages) that outlines the dispute, dates, key events, who did what, and desired outcome.
- Identify and contact potential witnesses; secure their contact info and ask them to preserve records.
- Mark upcoming court dates and deadlines in your calendar. Ask Maria to confirm them in writing.
- Avoid discussing the case publicly or with the opposing party without counsel present.
- If you think evidence may be destroyed, ask Maria about an immediate preservation letter to opposing counsel or third parties.

How to organize materials for Maria
- Create a single secure folder (cloud or encrypted) with subfolders: Contracts, Compensation, Communications, HR, Financials, Witnesses, Timeline, Court Papers.
- Name files clearly (YYYYMMDD_subject_description.pdf) and include an index spreadsheet listing files and short descriptions.
- Highlight the highest-priority documents in the folder and point Maria to them.

Questions about costs/timeline
- Ask Maria for an estimated timeline from now to discovery to trial and a projected budget or cost ranges for document collection, expert retention, depositions, and trial prep.

Sample short email to Maria you can send (editable)
