Good — I can help you get organized for your meeting with Maria Santos. Below are focused questions to ask, a prioritized checklist of evidence to gather/preserve, what to expect at the preliminary hearing, and suggested next steps. If you’d like, I can also draft an email or a short script you can use when you call or meet Maria.

Questions to ask your attorney (Maria Santos)
- Case basics & deadlines
  - What is the exact deadline to respond to the complaint? What happens if we miss it (default, extensions)?
  - Should we file an Answer, a Motion to Dismiss, or something else first?
  - Is there any local rule or Maricopa County deadline I need to know about for the preliminary hearing?

- Purpose of the preliminary hearing
  - What is the court likely to decide or schedule at the preliminary hearing (temporary orders, case timeline, bifurcation, discovery schedule)?
  - Do I need to bring any affidavits, exhibits, or declarations to that hearing?

- Evidence: what is needed now vs. later
  - Which specific documents or pieces of evidence are essential to have before the preliminary hearing?
  - Which evidence can be gathered during discovery (requests for production, interrogatories, depositions)?
  - Do we need to retain any experts (e.g., damages, accounting) before the preliminary hearing?
  - Are there any documents or communications we should prioritize obtaining/serving subpoenas for now?

- Preservation and authenticity
  - What immediate steps should I take to preserve evidence (emails, texts, voicemails, work records, financials)?
  - How should I preserve electronic evidence (screenshots, export emails, preserve metadata)?
  - Do we need a litigation hold notice to former colleagues or third parties?

- Witnesses
  - Which witnesses should I identify now (co-workers, clients, contractors, accountants)?
  - Do we need written statements or declarations from them before the hearing?

- Discovery strategy & timing
  - What discovery should we serve first (requests for production, interrogatories, requests for admission)?
  - When can we schedule depositions or serve subpoenas? Will the court set a discovery schedule at the preliminary hearing?
  - Any likely objections/opportunities to file motions (protective orders, motions to compel, motions in limine)?

- Legal strategy & settlement
  - What are the strengths/weaknesses of our case? What outcome is realistic at settlement vs. trial?
  - Should we propose mediation or settlement talks now?
  - Would a continuance of the preliminary hearing be advisable if we need more time to gather evidence?

- Costs & timing
  - What is your estimated timeline to trial and estimated costs if we proceed?
  - What are likely out-of-pocket costs I should expect for collecting evidence, experts, depositions?

- Practical questions about communication & involvement
  - How often will you update me? How should I send new evidence to you (secure portal/email)?
  - Is anything I say on social media or to third parties likely to hurt the case? What should I avoid saying or doing?

Prioritized evidence checklist (what to gather and bring to Maria)
Immediate (do these now)
- Complaint and proof of service documents from the court
- Any documents that form the contract(s) in dispute (signed agreements, amendments)
- All communications about the contract: emails, texts, messages, internal memos, meeting notes (export and save originals)
- Payment records, invoices, payroll records, bank statements, canceled checks related to the contract
- Performance records showing how/when obligations were met or not met
- Any letters, termination notices, or warnings from the employer
- Calendar entries and important dates (when contract was signed, performance dates, termination date)
- Any offers, settlement communications, or prior legal correspondence

Before preliminary hearing (if available)
- Chronology/timeline (one-page timeline of key events and supporting docs)
- List of potential witnesses with contact info and short summary of their expected testimony
- Any supporting documents for damages or losses (ledgers, profit/loss, lost wages)
- Relevant HR records or personnel files (if applicable and lawful to obtain)

If case moves toward trial
- Expert reports or names of experts (accountant, vocational expert, damages expert)
- Detailed witness statements or declarations
- Backup copies of all electronically stored information with metadata preserved
- Copies of subpoenas and responses from third parties

Preservation steps you should take right away
- Do not delete any emails, texts, voicemail, social media posts, or files related to the dispute
- Export or save emails (PDF or EML), take screenshots of texts with date/time, and back up copies to cloud/external drive
- Put relevant devices in a safe place and avoid factory resets or deletion
- If applicable, ask Maria about sending a formal litigation hold to relevant custodians (former co-workers, HR)
- Avoid discussing case details with the opposing party or posting about it publicly

What to expect at the preliminary hearing
- Often the court will set a case schedule (discovery deadlines, motion cutoffs, pretrial conference)
- The judge may rule on temporary relief (injunctive relief, temporary custody of documents, attachments) if requested
- May discuss ADR/mediation and set deadlines
- It’s less likely to be a full evidentiary hearing unless there’s an urgent temporary relief issue

Discovery and evidence admissibility considerations to ask about
- How we will authenticate key documents (business records exception, witness authentication)
- What additional steps we should take to secure electronic evidence and metadata
- Whether hearsay issues might come up and how to handle them (affidavits vs. live testimony)
- Whether we should obtain subpoenas duces tecum now for third-party records (banks, platforms)

Practical items to bring to your meeting with Maria
- Printed copy of the complaint and all court documents
- The timeline document and a folder of the immediate evidence items above
- A short written list of questions/priorities (so you don’t forget in the meeting)
- Contact info for potential witnesses and any third parties who hold documents

Sample opening lines/questions you can use when you call or email Maria
- “Maria — I’ve been served and my response is due on [date]. Do we need to file an Answer or should we move to dismiss? What are the immediate next steps?”
- “What evidence do you need from me before the preliminary hearing on [date]? I can gather documents and witness contacts; what should I prioritize?”
- “Should we send a litigation hold to anyone? Are there third-party records we should subpoena now?”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email to Maria with the above questions and a summary of what evidence you have (I can draft it for you now)
- Create the one-page timeline/chronology from the facts you give me
- Make a tailored evidence checklist based on what you already have and what you don’t
- Draft a short script for your preliminary hearing or a list of items to bring to the hearing

Would you like me to:
1) Draft an email to Maria with these questions? If yes, give me any dates or the response deadline and any evidence you already have; or
2) Build a one-page timeline/chronology from the facts you provide now?

