Good — this is exactly the kind of prep that helps your attorney use your time efficiently. Below are focused questions and prompts you can take to Maria Santos, organized so you cover deadlines, evidence needs, preservation, discovery strategy, witnesses, and logistics. I also include a prioritized checklist of documents/evidence to gather before the next date and sample phrasing you can use.

Start by confirming the basics with Maria
- Can you confirm the exact next court date(s) and all filing deadlines (answer, motions, disclosures)? What date was I officially served, and when does my response deadline expire?
- If we need more time to respond or gather evidence, what are my options for requesting an extension and how likely is the court to grant it?

Key questions about evidence and whether you need more before the next date
- Based on the complaint and the current pleadings, what specific facts or documents do you think are essential for our response and for opposing their claims?
- Which pieces of evidence should we absolutely have before the preliminary hearing (versus what can be gathered during discovery)?
- Are there gaps in the record right now that would hurt our position at the hearing if not filled?
- Do you recommend we obtain sworn statements or affidavits from any witnesses before the hearing?
- Are there any documents or data we should subpoena now (employer records, third-party communications, bank records)? If so, who should we subpoena and how fast can that be done?
- Should we be considering immediate preservation letters or litigation hold notices to third parties (employer, banks, social media providers)?

Evidence collection — concrete items to ask for and bring
- Employment-related: employment contract(s), offer letter, termination notice, severance agreements, performance reviews, promotion/demotion memos, payroll records, benefit documents.
- Communications: all emails, direct messages, text messages, and voicemail transcripts involving the dispute (with dates). Include any communications with your ex-spouse about the contract/employment.
- Financial records: pay stubs, bank statements, invoices, expense reports, reimbursements, proof of payments or losses claimed.
- Documents showing performance or work product relevant to the contract/dispute (deliverables, meeting notes, project timelines).
- Third-party evidence: witness contact info, statements, or corroborating emails from coworkers, supervisors, clients.
- Copies of any contract amendments, NDAs, or side agreements.
- Anything you think may have been deleted — note where it was and when.

Preservation and handling of evidence
- Ask Maria to send preservation/litigation-hold instructions immediately — should I stop deleting any emails/texts/documents and preserve device backups?
- Should I make copies of original documents, and do you want originals or certified copies? How should I label and deliver them?
- What about metadata and authenticating electronic evidence — do we need forensic images or timestamps preserved?

Discovery strategy and timing
- What discovery tools should we use first (requests for production, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas)?
- What is our timeline for serving discovery and responding to theirs?
- If we need documents from the employer or other third parties, when should we issue subpoenas to get them in time?
- Do you recommend any immediate forensic work (email forensics, phone extraction) and how long does that take?

Witnesses and testimony
- Who should we consider as witnesses and what will each witness be asked to prove?
- Do you recommend written declarations from witnesses before the hearing?
