Good — that’s a clear goal. Below is a focused, practical checklist of questions and tasks you can use with Maria Santos to determine whether you need more evidence before the next hearing. I’ll also include immediate action items you can do now and a short sample message you can send your attorney.

Key categories of questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the exact next court date, and what is that hearing’s purpose (preliminary, scheduling, temporary orders, etc.)?
  - What are the hard deadlines I must meet before that date (response to complaint, disclosures, exhibit lists)?
  - Are there any local Maricopa County or Arizona-specific rules I should know about for this stage?
- Evidence sufficiency & priorities
  - Based on the complaint/claims, what evidence do you consider essential to have before the hearing?
  - Which documents or facts would most strongly support my contract-dispute claims/defenses (contract, emails, payment records, performance records, termination/severance documents)?
  - Which items are lower priority and can wait until later discovery?
- Preservation & scope
  - What steps should I take right now to preserve evidence (emails, texts, files, social media, devices)?
  - Do we need a formal litigation hold or a written preservation notice to opposing counsel?
  - Are there any privacy or privilege concerns with certain documents (attorney communications, personal records)?
- Witnesses & statements
  - Who should we identify now as potential witnesses (co-workers, supervisors, HR, third parties)?
  - Should we get written witness statements or affidavits before the hearing?
  - Do any witnesses need to be subpoenaed or prepared for deposition immediately?
- Discovery & evidence collection tools
  - Should we serve discovery requests or subpoenas now (documents, bank records, employer records)?
  - Do we need an expert (e.g., damages, accounting, employment expert) at this stage?
  - Are there forensic needs (metadata preservation, device imaging) for electronic evidence?
- Exhibits & courtroom readiness
  - What exhibits should I bring to the preliminary hearing? How should they be organized and labeled?
  - Will the court expect a pre-hearing exhibit list or pretrial statement?
  - Will the hearing be limited to oral argument, or might evidence be presented?
- Temporary relief & urgent motions
  - Should we seek any temporary relief now (temporary injunction, restraining order, temporary support, asset preservation)?
  - If so, what evidence is needed to support an emergency motion?
- Settlement & ADR
  - Is settlement/mediation a realistic option before the hearing? If so, what evidence strengthens our negotiating position?
  - Should any communications be preserved for settlement talks but kept privileged?
- Costs, timeline & strategy
  - What is the expected timeline and likely next steps after the preliminary hearing?
  - What are the estimated costs and time for additional discovery (depositions, experts)?
  - How will additional evidence alter strategy or the likelihood of moving to full trial?

Immediate action items (what you can do right away)
- Preserve everything: stop deleting or altering emails, texts, social media posts, files, and calendars. Back up devices (phone, laptop).
- Create a chronological timeline of key events (dates, times, who was involved, short description).
- Gather core documents now:
  - Contract(s), amendments, offer letters, termination/severance letters
  - Emails, text messages, Slack or other workplace messages
  - HR records, performance reviews, pay stubs, payroll records
  - Any written policies or workplace manuals referenced in the dispute
  - Bank statements or payment records showing damages or payments
- Identify and list potential witnesses with contact info and a short statement of what each can attest to.
- Take screenshots of any web or social evidence (include date/time if visible) and save originals.
- If you have voicemail or call logs relevant to the case, save/export them.
- Note any deadlines/filings received (date served, by whom) and give copies to your attorney.

Practical, quick questions to ask Maria (one-sentence prompts)
- “Given the upcoming hearing, which three pieces of evidence should I prioritize collecting?”
- “Do you recommend we get written witness statements or affidavits now?”
- “Should I preserve/produce all emails and texts or only those related to the contract?”
- “Do we need any expert reports before the hearing?”
- “Do you want me to send you a digital folder of all documents now? If so, what format/structure do you prefer?”
- “Are there immediate discovery steps you want me to authorize (subpoenas, preservation letters)?”

Red flags to mention to your attorney
- Any sign the opposing party is destroying or withholding evidence
- Missing key documents that only the employer or third party holds (HR files, payroll, emails)
- Short statutory deadlines or imminent hearing dates
- Conflicting prior statements or documents that need quick clarification

