I’m not a lawyer, but I can help you organize targeted questions to ask Maria Santos so you get clear advice on whether you need more evidence before the next court date. Below is a prioritized list you can use in a call, meeting, or email — grouped so it’s easy to follow — plus a short checklist of documents to gather and a ready-to-send sample message to your attorney.

Immediate / procedural (ask first)
- What is the exact deadline for my response (answer, motion, or other) and any upcoming court dates I must be aware of?
- What is the purpose of the preliminary hearing and what standard or issues will the court decide then?
- Given the short window, do we need to file anything before the preliminary hearing (response, motion, request for continuance)?
- Are there any local Maricopa County rules or special procedures I must follow before the next date?
- If we need more time to collect evidence, can we request a continuance and how likely is it to be granted?

Evidence and relevance (core questions)
- Which specific elements of my contract-dispute claim/defense do we need evidence for now? (Ask Maria to map facts to legal elements.)
- What types of evidence will be most persuasive for those elements (contracts, pay records, emails, texts, performance reviews, signed forms, witness testimony, payroll records)?
- Are there weaknesses in the evidence we already have? What gaps should I prioritize filling?
- What evidence would the opposing party likely produce and how should we respond?

Preservation and immediate collection steps
- What must I preserve right now (emails, phone messages, texts, work files, backups)? Should I send a written preservation notice to any third parties (employer, IT, witnesses)?
- Should I make forensic copies (native files, metadata) of emails/phone records or are PDFs/screenshots sufficient at this stage?
- Who should I avoid contacting (or what should I avoid posting on social media) to protect privilege and strategy?

Discovery options and timing
- What discovery tools should we use to get more evidence (document requests, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas to employer or third parties, subpoenas for bank records)?
- Will the court allow expedited discovery before the preliminary hearing if needed?
- How long do subpoenas and third-party records requests typically take here, and when should we serve them?

Witnesses and testimony
- Which witnesses should we talk to now and what questions should we ask them?
- Should we take witness statements or sworn declarations now? If so, how should they be drafted?
- Do we need expert witness support (e.g., damages expert, employment expert)? If yes, when do we retain them?

Authentication and admissibility
- What proof will we need to authenticate documents (emails with headers, server logs, chain of custody)?
- Are there any hearsay or privilege issues we should anticipate with records or witness statements?
- How do we handle records held by my former employer (HR files, payroll, personnel notes)?

Strategy and risks
- Given our current evidence, what are our realistic strengths and weaknesses?
- What is the recommended short-term strategy before the preliminary hearing (settlement attempt, narrow focus, aggressive evidence collection)?
- What are the likely motions the opposing party could file and how should we prepare?

Logistics and cost/benefit
- What are the expected costs and timeline to obtain key evidence (depositions, subpoenas, experts)?
- Which evidence should we prioritize if we have to limit spending?
- Would attempting focused settlement talks now be beneficial before incurring more costs?

Practical next steps / assignments
- Who (you or attorney) will take responsibility for each action (e.g., preservation letter, subpoena, contacting witnesses)?
- When should I check back with you and in what format (email, phone, in-person)?
- What documents or summaries would be most useful to provide you immediately?

What to bring or prepare for your next communication/meeting
- All employment-related documents (signed contract(s), offer letters, termination notice, NDAs, performance reviews, pay stubs, time sheets)
- All communications (emails, text messages, WhatsApp/other messages, LinkedIn/InMail) with dates — export native files if possible
- HR or company policies that relate to the dispute
- Bank/transaction records or invoices showing damages/payments
- Names and contact details of potential witnesses and a short description of what each saw or knows
- A one-page chronology of key events with dates (helps attorney pinpoint gaps)
- Any prior pleadings, summons, service documents you received

How to organize evidence (quick checklist)
- Make a single secure folder (local + backup) and subfolders by type: Contracts, Emails, Texts, Payroll, HR, Witnesses, Financials, Court Documents.
- Save native files when possible (e.g., .eml/.pst for emails) to preserve metadata; if not, create full-page PDFs and save screenshots with date/time visible.
- Keep an evidence log (filename, description, date, source, why it matters).
- Do not delete anything and avoid cleaning devices until Maria advises.

Sample message you can send Maria Santos
Hi Maria — I’m preparing for the upcoming preliminary hearing in my Maricopa County civil case (contract dispute with my former employer/ex-spouse). I’m concerned about whether we need more evidence before the next date and what immediate steps I should take. Can we schedule a quick call or meeting? Below is a short list of items I want to cover:
- Exact deadlines and whether we need to file anything now
- Evidence gaps you see and what to prioritize collecting
- Immediate preservation steps (emails, texts, backups) and whether to send preservation letters
- Discovery options and timelines (subpoenas, depositions)
- Witnesses and expert needs
I can bring the attached chronology and documents. Please let me know the earliest available time and whether you want me to start any preservation steps before our call. Thanks, [Your Name]

Final suggestions
- Ask Maria to prioritize actions you must take personally (preservation, witness contact) vs. things the firm will handle (subpoenas, filings).
- If you want, I can turn the question list into a one-page meeting agenda or a polished email tailored to Maria’s firm — tell me whether you want a short email or a longer agenda and I’ll draft it.

Would you like me to draft that email to Maria now (short or detailed)?

