I can help you get organized. Below is a practical, lawyer-focused checklist and a prioritized list of specific questions to ask Maria Santos so you can determine whether you need more evidence before the next date. I also include immediate action items you should do now and an offer to draft an email or call script to your lawyer.

Quick note: I’m not a lawyer. This is organizational/preparatory help — use it to guide your conversation with Maria Santos (Santos Law Office), who can give legal advice tailored to your case.

1) What to bring to your next meeting/hearing (documents to gather now)
- Complaint and any summons, plus the proof of service you received
- Any responses you’ve already drafted or filed (or drafts)
- All written contracts, amendments, and signed pages related to the dispute
- Emails, text messages, DMs, voicemail transcripts, and dated communications with your former employer and ex-spouse about the contract
- Payment records, invoices, bank statements, payroll records, canceled checks
- Time-stamped files, logs, calendars showing relevant dates/meetings
- Performance reviews, HR records, policies, memos that relate to the contract terms
- Witness contact info (names, phone/email) and short notes on what each can say
- Photos, photos metadata, recordings, video files (with original files/backups)
- Any earlier settlement offers, negotiation notes, or released documents
- Social media posts/screenshots relevant to the dispute (with date/time info)
- Anything you’re worried might be lost (emails on old accounts, devices)

2) Immediate preservation steps (do these now; then tell your attorney)
- Preserve all relevant ESI (emails, files, texts); don’t delete anything.
- Back up devices and relevant accounts to an external drive or cloud storage.
- Turn off auto-delete on apps/accounts that may purge messages.
- Save conversation transcripts/screenshots in original format where possible.
- If you think evidence could be destroyed, tell Maria about a preservation letter or other steps she recommends.

3) Practical questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped by topic)

Case status and deadlines
- What is the exact next court date and what will happen at that hearing (preliminary/settlement/ADR/case management)?
- What are the immediate court deadlines I must meet (response to complaint, initial disclosures, etc.) and their dates?
- How much time do we have to collect evidence before the next deadlines?

Strategy & burden
- What is our legal theory/element(s) we must prove for the contract claim/defense? What specific evidence will satisfy those elements?
- Based on what we already have, are there obvious evidentiary gaps that could jeopardize our position at the next hearing or at trial?

Evidence gathering & priorities
- Which documents and witnesses are highest priority to obtain before the next date?
- Should we obtain sworn witness statements (affidavits) before the hearing? From whom?
- Do we need any expert reports or valuations? If so, who (forensic accountant, damages expert) and how long will they take/cost?
- Are there any third parties (banks, vendors, HR) we need records from? What’s the best way to get them (subpoena, voluntary request)?
- Are there digital evidence issues (metadata, deleted files) that require immediate forensic preservation/collection?

Discovery & motions
- What discovery tools should we use now (requests for production, interrogatories, subpoenas, depositions)?
- Is there enough time to propound and enforce discovery before the next date?
- Should we file a motion now (for extension, emergency relief, or preservation) to protect our evidence or buy time?
- Would a Motion in Limine, protective order, or temporary injunction be relevant at this stage?

Admissibility & chain-of-custody
- Are there any likely objections to key evidence (hearsay, authenticity, privilege)? How do we authenticate important documents?
- How should we preserve chain-of-custody for physical or digital evidence?

Opposing party / tactical questions
- What evidence is the ex-spouse likely to produce? Are there inconsistencies we can exploit?
- Are there settlement prospects? Is settlement before discovery advisable, or should we wait to gather more evidence?

Costs & logistics
- What are estimated costs and timelines for subpoenas, expert reports, depositions, and forensic work?
- What are the likely trade-offs between moving fast to the hearing vs. asking the court for more time to gather evidence?

Court & procedure specifics (Maricopa County, AZ)
- Any local rules or procedures in the Maricopa County civil division I should know about (e.g., page limits, exhibit requirements, hearing format)?
- Will the hearing be in-person or remote? Are there special exhibit/electronic filing rules?

Client behavior & protection
- What should I say or not say publicly or to mutual contacts? Can I communicate with the ex-spouse directly or only through counsel?
- Should I notify or place a litigation hold with my former employer or other organizations?

Exhibit & presentation prep
- How should we prepare exhibits (format, labeling, copies for court/opposing counsel)?
- What witness preparation is required and when should we schedule it?

Fallback planning
- If the court denies a request for more time, what is our contingency plan? If we can’t gather X evidence, what theories remain viable?

4) Suggested short script to open the conversation with Maria Santos
“Hi Maria — I want to prepare effectively for the upcoming preliminary hearing in the Maricopa County civil division. I’ve assembled documents and want your guidance on what additional evidence is essential before the next date and what we should prioritize collecting now. Specifically: what are the most important documents and witnesses we need, whether we should obtain sworn statements or an expert before the hearing, and whether you recommend filing any preservation or extension motions. I can bring the files I have and would like to set a timeline and task list.”

5) Prioritized action checklist (what to do in the next 48–72 hours)
- Tell Maria Santos you need immediate advice and provide copies of the complaint and proof of service.
- Preserve and back up all relevant ESI and physical documents.
