  - Is jurisdiction and venue correct (Maricopa County)? Was I properly served — any defects to challenge?
  - Are there contract terms or clauses (choice of law, forum selection, arbitration, liquidated damages) that could change strategy?
  - Do we have any statute of limitations or timing issues relevant to counterclaims or cross-claims?

B. Evidence checklist — what to collect and preserve now (prioritize immediate preservation)
- Contracts and agreements
  - Original contract(s), amendments, addenda, signed pages, drafts, and any executed exhibits or schedules.
  - Any written acceptance, acknowledgment, or termination notices.

- Communications
  - All emails between you and the opposing party and/or former employer about the contract or performance.
  - Text messages, WhatsApp, Messenger, social media messages/comments related to the dispute.
  - Voicemails or call logs that are relevant (export or record them).

- Financial and transactional records
  - Bank statements, canceled checks, wire transfers, invoices, receipts, payroll records, tax documents showing payments or lack thereof.
  - Expense reports, reimbursements, or credit card statements tied to the contract.

- Employment / company records (since a former employer is involved)
  - Employment agreement, job descriptions, HR records, performance reviews, termination letter(s), internal memos, payroll records.
  - Any communications with HR or supervisors addressing the contract or the contested events.

- Documents demonstrating damages (if damages claimed)
  - Accounting ledgers, profit/loss statements, invoices to third parties, evidence of lost business or profits, receipts for mitigation costs.

- Metadata and originals
  - Preserve original files (Word docs, PDFs, emails) with metadata intact. Export emails as .eml or .msg if possible.
  - Keep originals or certified copies where possible; take high-quality scans and backups.

- Physical evidence and exhibits
  - Photographs, signed hard-copy documents, original receipts, certificates, logs, calendar entries, handwritten notes, notebooks.

- Third-party records
  - Records from banks, cloud storage providers, employers, vendors, or platforms (subpoenaable if needed). Note who holds critical evidence.

- Witness info
  - Names, contact info, and summary of testimony for any witnesses (coworkers, supervisors, contractors, clients).
  - Any contemporaneous notes they made, or statements they already gave.

- Timeline and chronology
  - Create a dated timeline (chronology) of key events with references to the supporting document IDs/pages.

C. Immediate preservation steps (do these right away)
- Do not delete any emails, messages, files, or devices. Stop auto-deleting and back everything up.
- Export important emails (save as .eml/.msg or PDF with metadata). Screenshot texts and export message threads; preserve original devices.
- Make duplicates and store one copy offsite or in encrypted cloud storage.
- If you suspect evidence is held by a third party (bank, employer), tell your attorney immediately about who to subpoena and when.
- Keep a written log of who touched or altered any document or device after preservation began.

D. Prioritization for the upcoming preliminary hearing (what to have ready soon)
- Proof of service and the complaint/pleadings.
- Your timeline and a short summary of your position (one page).
- The most critical documents that prove or refute the central elements of the claim (e.g., the signed contract, key emails showing intent/performance, payment records).
- Names of 2–3 key witnesses and short witness statements/affidavits if possible.
- Any emergency relief you want the judge to consider (and the facts supporting it).

E. Questions about resources & costs to ask Maria
- What will be the estimated cost for immediate discovery (document collection, subpoenas, depositions, expert reports)?
- Do we need to retain experts now and what is the cost/time to prepare a usable report?
- Are advance payments or retainers required for upcoming tasks?

F. Practical tips for your meeting with Maria
- Bring a printed timeline and folder of the top 10 most important documents.
- Bring device access or exported files (USB or secure cloud) so counsel can immediately review metadata.
- Ask Maria to identify “dealbreaker” pieces of evidence — things we must secure now at all costs.

