I can help you get organized. I’m not a lawyer, so this is general guidance — use it to structure your conversation with Maria Santos and to make sure you’ve covered the bases before the next court date.

1) Quick prep you should do right away
- Preserve everything: don’t delete emails/texts/voicemails/files that relate to the job, termination, and dispute. Make backups (cloud + local).
- Save originals and create digital copies (PDFs). Note date/time and where each item came from.
- Put a short chronology together (dates and one-line description of key events).
- Write down potential witnesses (name, contact, what they saw/know).
- Note any deadlines or service dates you’ve been given and bring those to your meeting.

2) Key questions to ask Maria (phrased for conversation)
- What are the legal elements we must prove in this contract dispute and which elements are most likely to be contested?
- Given the preliminary hearing date, do we need additional evidence now, or can it wait until later discovery/trial?
- Which documents or categories of evidence are essential for the preliminary hearing versus for trial?
- Do we need to serve discovery requests (interrogatories, requests for production) or subpoenas before the hearing? If so, what specifically should we request and when?
- Are there urgent preservation steps or affidavits I should get now to prevent spoliation?
- Should we seek expedited discovery, a protective order, or any pre-hearing motions?
- Do you recommend obtaining any expert (e.g., forensic accountant, employment expert)? If so, when would that expert need time to prepare?
- What is our plan for witness testimony at the preliminary hearing—live testimony, declarations, or relying on exhibits?
- Are there potential admissibility issues with the evidence I have (authentication, hearsay, privilege)?
- What is the likelihood we will settle before trial and what discovery/materials are useful to support a settlement position?
- What are the next procedural deadlines (disclosures, discovery cutoff, pretrial conference) and can we get a written calendar?
- Do you recommend I request a continuance/extension to gather evidence? What is the process and likely outcome?
- What costs should I expect for obtaining records, expert reports, or depositions?
- How should I label and organize documents/exhibits so they’re trial-ready? Do you prefer a binder, digital folder, or both?

3) Documents and evidence to gather (practical checklist)
- Contract(s) and any amendments, offer letters, change orders, or written job descriptions
- Termination/discipline letters, performance reviews, warnings, and HR records
- All emails and text messages relevant to hiring, job duties, performance, termination, and communications with the opposing party
- Timecards/paystubs, benefits or compensation records, expense reports, bonuses, commission statements
- Company policies, employee handbook, and any dispute resolution or arbitration clauses
- Notes/journal entries you made contemporaneously (dates and short descriptions)
- Witness contact info and brief notes of what each witnessed
- Any financial documents relevant to claims (bank records, invoices)
- Photos/screenshots/CCTV or other audiovisual materials (with metadata where possible)
- Prior pleadings, summons, proof of service and any court orders already filed

4) Evidence-specific questions to ask about each item
- Who can authenticate this document? (Who will testify that it’s genuine?)
- Is any item subject to privilege or confidentiality? How should we handle that?
- Do we need originals or are certified copies acceptable?
- Will metadata be important? Should we provide native files?

5) Strategy & admissibility concerns to raise
- Ask Maria about the strongest and weakest parts of your case and which evidence attacks credibility vs proves elements.
- Ask what the opposing side is likely to try to exclude and how to pre-empt that (foundation, chain of custody, witness statements).
- Ask whether any documents require redaction or protective arrangements (sensitive personal or third-party information).

6) Discovery & subpoenas (what to check)
- What targeted records should we subpoena (employer personnel file, payroll, internal communications)?
- How long does it take to get those records from third parties? (This affects whether you need to move for expedited discovery.)
- If records are held electronically, do we need a forensic image or just native exports?

7) Witnesses & testimony
- Who should be deposed or interviewed before the hearing? What are their likely recollections?
- Do we need affidavits/declarations if witnesses can’t attend the hearing?
- How to prepare witnesses: timeline, potential cross-examination, exhibits.

8) Costs, timeline, and logistics
- Ask for an estimated timeline from now through the preliminary hearing and likely trial if not resolved.
- Ask for a rough cost estimate for obtaining outstanding records, hiring experts, and depositions.
- Confirm how you’ll communicate and review documents (secure portal, email, in-person).

9) How to organize material for Maria
- Create a chronological index (date, document title, short description) and separate folders:
  - Core contract & employment docs
  - Communications (emails, texts)
  - Financials (payroll, bank)
  - Witness list & statements
  - Court papers (pleadings, service)
- Label each file with date and short title; produce PDFs named like YYYYMMDD_description.pdf
- Consider a one-page case summary for Maria (key facts, timeline, desired outcome).

