Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — I’ll keep this focused on preparing you to ask Maria whether you need more evidence before the next hearing and what to prioritize now. This is general guidance to help you organize; your lawyer will make final legal decisions.

Quick priorities (if you have a short window)
- Confirm the response/deadline and court date with Maria immediately.
- Issue a litigation hold: stop deleting any documents, texts, emails, voicemails, or other records related to the dispute.
- Gather the most important documents now (see checklist below).
- Prepare a short chronology of events (one page timeline) so your lawyer can quickly assess gaps.

Suggested direct questions to ask Maria Santos
(Use these exactly or as prompts in a meeting/email)
1. What is the exact deadline for responding and what should we file (Answer, motion, request for extension)?  
2. For the upcoming preliminary hearing, what is its purpose (status conference, scheduling, temporary relief)? What should I expect to happen?  
3. Based on our theory of the case, what specific evidence will be most important at this stage? What is low priority?  
4. Do we need to collect additional documents or witness statements before the next date? If so, which ones should I focus on first?  
5. Are there any time‑sensitive actions I must take now (subpoena, preservation letter to third parties, expert retention)?  
6. Should I get written/ sworn statements from specific witnesses now? If yes, who and what should they include?  
7. Do we need any immediate forensic preservation of electronic devices, cloud accounts, or metadata?  
8. Are there confidentiality or privilege issues I should be aware of when collecting or sharing documents?  
9. What discovery will we request first (documents, interrogatories, depositions) and when will those be served?  
10. Is settlement/mediation an option before trial? If so, do I need evidence now to support a settlement demand?  
11. What is the budget and timeline for obtaining experts or forensic work if needed?  
12. Do you recommend sending a preservation/hold notice to the opposing party or third parties?  
13. Is there anything I should NOT do or post (social media, speaking to certain people) before next hearing?  

Document/evidence checklist — gather or preserve these now
- Contract(s) and any amendments, offer letters, employee handbook or policies referenced in contract.  
- All written communications: emails, text messages, instant messages, DMs, letters between you and employer/ex-spouse relating to the contract or dispute. Preserve originals and export copies (PDFs).  
- HR records: performance reviews, disciplinary notices, termination notices, benefits records, payroll records.  
- Paystubs, invoices, expense reports, bank statements showing payments or losses tied to the dispute.  
- Any written notices, demands, or prior legal correspondence you received or sent.  
- Witness contact info and short notes on what each witness can testify to. Consider obtaining short written witness statements for key witnesses.  
- Calendar entries, meeting invitations, audio/video recordings (if lawful to keep/use in your jurisdiction), and location logs relevant to dates in dispute.  
- Contracts or communications showing your ex-spouse’s involvement in the workplace actions (if relevant).  
- Documents supporting damages (loss calculations, expert reports, tax records).  
- Copies of anything you already produced or were served (summons, complaint, exhibits).  
- Screenshots or exported metadata for social media posts or deleted messages (don’t alter originals).  

Preservation and ESI (electronic evidence) — immediate steps
- Put all relevant devices/accounts on hold — don’t delete emails/texts or factory-reset phones/computers.  
- Export and save email folders and important chats as PDF or PST; take screenshots with timestamps if needed.  
- Back up original files to an external drive or secure cloud in unaltered form.  
- Note when and how items were preserved (chain of custody). Tell Maria if you suspect anyone may have deleted or altered evidence.  

Questions specifically about evidence sufficiency and priorities
- Given time constraints, what 3–5 items would most strengthen our position before the next date?  
- What gaps in the evidence would force us to change strategy (e.g., need for an expert, missing contract terms, no corroborating witnesses)?  
- Are witness statements or depositions urgent now, or can they wait until after initial scheduling?  
- Which items require subpoenas and how long will that take here in Maricopa County?  
- If we can’t get more evidence before the next hearing, what procedural moves do you recommend (continuance, bring up at status hearing)?  

What to bring to the preliminary hearing or first meeting with Maria
- One-page timeline of key events and dates.  
- Binder or folder with labeled copies of the items above (contract, complaint, communications, HR file).  
- List of witnesses with contact info and short note on their expected testimony.  
- Any proposed exhibits you think are important.  
- Notes of any interactions with the opposing party since service.  

Organizing evidence for your lawyer
- Create a single folder (physical or encrypted cloud) labeled “Case – [short name]” with subfolders: Contracts, Communications (email/texts), HR/Payroll, Financial/Damages, Witnesses, Court Filings.  
- Number and index exhibits (Exhibit 1, 2, etc.) and include a short description and date.  
- Keep originals safe and give Maria copies in the format she prefers.

Practical tips / do nots
- Do not intentionally delete or alter anything — that can lead to sanctions.  
- Avoid posting about the case on social media or discussing with the opposing party without counsel present.  
- Don’t try to “improve” or rewrite documents; disclose originals and explain context to Maria.

Offer
- I can draft a short email you can send to Maria listing the above questions and attaching a one-page timeline. Would you like me to draft that now? If yes, tell me whether you want a formal or informal tone and any specific items you already have so I can include them.

