Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help you get organized and prepare a focused list of questions to ask Maria Santos about whether you need more evidence before the next court date. Below is a practical, prioritized checklist you can use at the meeting or in an email — with suggested question wording, what documents/evidence to gather now, and immediate preservation steps.

Quick note: I’m not your lawyer. These are organizational suggestions — ask Maria for legal advice tailored to your case.

1) Immediate procedural questions (first things to confirm)
- “What is the exact deadline for my response and any other upcoming filing deadlines?”  
- “What is the purpose of the upcoming preliminary hearing (scheduling/status/temporary orders)? What should I expect?”  
- “Do we need to ask the court for a continuance or emergency relief before the preliminary hearing?”  
- “Are there any immediate actions that will affect my rights (e.g., risk of default, temporary orders, asset restraints)?”

2) Evidence & discovery scope (what you need to know to decide if you need more evidence)
- “What facts/elements does the opposing party need to prove, and which elements do we need evidence for?”  
- “What evidence do you currently think is strong for our position? Where are the gaps?”  
- “What specific documents, communications, or witnesses are most important to support my claims/defenses?”  
- “What discovery tools should we use (written discovery, subpoenas, depositions, requests for production, interrogatories)? Which should we prioritize before the hearing?”  
- “Are there statutory or local rules in Maricopa County or Arizona that limit timing or scope of discovery for the preliminary phase?”

3) Documents and records to gather now (practical checklist)
Gather originals or best available copies and create a simple index (date, sender, recipient, brief description):
- Employment contract, offer letters, amendments, non-compete/non-disclosure agreements
- Termination letters, disciplinary notices, HR emails, resignation communications
- Paystubs, payroll records, bonus/commission records, benefit summaries
- Emails and text messages with employer, ex-spouse, and any relevant third parties (include attachments)
- Performance reviews, attendance records, work schedules, timesheets
- Contracts, invoices, purchase orders, meeting notes, internal memos relevant to the dispute
- Bank statements or payment records showing transfers relevant to the contract
- Any written agreements or communications with your ex-spouse about the disputed matters
- Court papers you’ve been served with (summons, complaint, any attachments)
- Notes of conversations (dates, who you spoke to, summary) and photographs, audio/video evidence
- Social media posts if they are relevant (capture and preserve screenshots with timestamps)
- Any previous settlement offers or negotiations

4) Evidence preservation and chain-of-custody
- “Should I send a preservation/legal hold notice to any third parties or the employer?”  
- “What steps should I take to preserve electronic evidence (emails, text messages, social media)? For how long?”  
- Immediate practical steps you can do now:
  - Don’t delete any relevant emails/texts/social posts.
  - Export/print emails and save native files (PDF and original if possible).
  - Take dated screenshots or export message threads for messaging apps.
  - Back up your devices (phone/computer) and save copies offline.
  - Make a log of when and how evidence was collected to show chain-of-custody.

5) Witnesses and statements
- “Who should we interview or depose before the hearing? Any witnesses who can corroborate dates, communications, or conduct?”  
- “Do we need written witness statements or affidavits now?”  
- “How do we handle witnesses who are reluctant or are third parties — do we want subpoenas?”

6) Experts and technical evidence
- “Do we need an expert (accountant, HR expert, damages expert) before the hearing or later at trial?”  
- “If we need an expert, how long will it take and what evidence will they require?”

7) Admissibility and authentication
- “Are there likely admissibility issues (hearsay, authentication, privilege) with my documents or communications? How can we address them now?”  
- “Do we need to prepare authorization releases for third-party records (bank, employer, phone provider) or a privilege log?”

8) Strategy & settlement posture
- “What is our litigation strategy for the preliminary hearing versus trial?”  
- “Should we engage in settlement talks now or wait until discovery?”  
- “What are the likely costs and timeline to trial, and how does that affect whether we should invest in more evidence now?”

9) Logistics, costs, and timelines
- “How much will additional discovery, expert work, or subpoenas cost and how long will each take?”  
- “What are the deadlines for serving discovery and taking depositions in Maricopa County?”  
- “Who will handle document collection and organization — can I do it, or will your office manage it?”

10) Communication and next steps
- “How do you want me to provide documents (secure portal, email, in-person drop-off) and what format?”  
- “What do you want me to prioritize collecting in the next 7 days?”  
- “When can we meet/talk next to review the materials before the hearing?”

Sample prioritized immediate actions to take (today/this week)
- Confirm the exact response and hearing dates with Maria.  
- Save and back up any relevant electronic communications (email, texts, social media posts).  
- Collect and make copies of employment contract, termination/HR documents, pay records, and the complaint you were served with.  
- Make a chronological timeline of key events (dates, what happened, who was involved). Share it with Maria.  
- Ask Maria whether she wants you to send a preservation notice to the former employer or any third parties.

How to present this to Maria (quick options)
- Bring a 1–2 page timeline and an indexed packet (or secure folder) with the most important documents.  
- Or send a short email: one-paragraph case summary, attached timeline, and “Top 8 documents attached — please tell me which additional items you want before the hearing.”

Would you like:
- A ready-to-send email to Maria with the timeline and questions above?  
- A checklist document you can print and use at the meeting?  
- Help drafting the preservation notice or an index of documents you already have?

Tell me which of those you want and whether you want the email phrased formally or casually. I can draft it for you.

