Good — I can help you prepare so your meeting with Maria Santos is focused and you don’t miss anything important before the next date. Below are (1) high-priority questions to ask your attorney at the next meeting, (2) follow-up / deeper questions if time allows, (3) a practical evidence checklist to assemble now, and (4) immediate preservation and organization steps. If you want, I can draft a short email or message to Maria with these questions or turn your documents into a timeline/exhibit list.

1) Must-ask questions (ask these first)
- What exactly is the next hearing (status conference, preliminary hearing, scheduling conference, evidentiary hearing)? What will be decided there?
- What is the firm deadline to file any response, answer, or other pleading? What are the court deadlines we must meet (federal/state/county rules and local rules for Maricopa County)?
- Do we need any additional evidence before the next date, or is the hearing primarily procedural?
- If we do need more evidence, which pieces are essential versus “nice to have” to preserve our position?
- What immediate preservation steps should I take (documents, devices, accounts)? Should I send a written preservation/legal hold notice to anyone (former employer, third parties)?
- Will we need to serve discovery (document requests, interrogatories, requests for admission, depositions) before the next hearing? If not now, when?
- Do we plan to seek subpoenas for documents or testimony? If so, who should we subpoena and when?
- Are there statute-of-limitations or other timing risks I should know about?
- What is the expected timeline and next steps after the hearing if the case proceeds to trial?
- Do we need an expert? If so, what kind, and when would they need materials?

2) Follow-up / deeper questions (if time allows)
- What legal elements do we need to prove (or defend) in the contract claim and what evidence supports each element?
- What is our burden of proof and how strong is the opposing side’s likely evidence?
- Are there any admissions or documents we should ask the opposing party to produce via RFA or document requests?
- Are there privilege or confidentiality issues with any documents (NDA, company confidential info, attorney-client, etc.)?
- What types of witnesses would be most persuasive (former supervisors, HR, coworkers, third-party vendors)?
- What are likely motions the other side may file (dismissal, summary judgment) and what evidence will counter them?
- Settlement posture: should we begin settlement discussions or mediation now?
- Costs and fees: estimated costs for discovery, experts, and depositions; do we have options to limit costs?

3) Evidence checklist — gather and preserve these now
Documents:
- The contract(s) at issue (original and all signed copies, amendments, addenda)
- Any offer letters, employment agreements, NDAs, severance agreements, or post-employment agreements
- All relevant communications: emails, texts, direct messages (work and personal if relevant), Slack/Teams messages, LinkedIn messages
- Performance records: evaluations, disciplinary notices, promotion or demotion records
- Payroll records, paystubs, bonuses, commission statements, benefits records, PTO records
- HR files and termination records, exit interview notes
- Any written policies, employee handbooks, internal memos relevant to the dispute
- Invoices, receipts, billing records, or other finance evidence if damages are monetary
- Calendars, meeting invites, notes from meetings or calls
- Any prior settlement or correspondence with the opposing party about the issue

People/witnesses:
- Names, contact info, and short notes about potential witnesses (what they can testify to)
- Any written witness statements or contemporaneous notes you or others made

Digital evidence and metadata:
- Original files (not just screenshots) with metadata when possible (emails, attachments)
- Export copies of chat logs, Slack, Teams, or social media posts
- Backups, archived mailboxes, or copies from old laptops/phones

Other:
- Photographs, recordings, or physical evidence (if any)
- Any official notices you were served (summons, complaint, exhibits) and proof of service

4) Immediate preservation & organizational steps (do these today)
- Preserve everything: stop deleting or altering emails, texts, files, calendars, and voicemails related to the dispute.
- Back up copies of relevant devices/accounts (email account export, save chat logs, copy files to a secure folder).
- Put together a single folder structure (electronic) with subfolders: Contracts, Communications, Payroll/Finance, HR, Witnesses, Court Filings.
- Create a simple chronological timeline (dates, short description, documents linked) — this helps your lawyer quickly see the story.
- If you haven’t already, ask Maria whether she wants you to send a short “preservation” email to former employer/IT/HR or whether she’ll handle legal hold letters.
- Lock down access: change passwords only after checking with your attorney if there’s any risk of appearing to alter evidence. Don’t delete accounts or records.
- Note any missing items and who might still have them (former HR, coworker, IT) so we can subpoena or request them quickly.

5) How to present this to Maria (quick message template — I can draft this for you)
- Offer to send a concise list of the documents you already have and ask if she wants scanned copies or originals.
- Ask for a very short call before the hearing (10–20 minutes) to confirm what is needed and the immediate deadlines.
- Ask if Maria wants you to begin any discovery steps or to wait for formal instruction.

6) Final tips
- Prioritize preservation and making a timeline — those are the fastest ways to add value before a hearing.
- Focus on evidence that proves the contract terms, breach, causation, and damages (or disproves the other side’s version).
- If you want, upload or list the specific documents you already have and I’ll help turn them into an exhibit checklist and a chronological timeline you can send to Maria.

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Maria with the must-ask questions and a request for a brief call? OR
- Turn your existing documents (tell me what you have) into a timeline and exhibit list you can hand to your lawyer?

