Good — I can help you get organized so your conversation with Maria is focused and productive. Below is a compact, prioritized plan plus specific questions to ask your attorney and a checklist of evidence to gather before the preliminary hearing.

Quick note: I’m not your lawyer. Use this list to guide your discussion with Maria Santos — she’ll tell you what’s legally required in your case and jurisdiction.

1) Immediate priorities (do these now)
- Confirm the exact service date and the preliminary hearing date. Ask Maria the deadline to file any response or motion and whether any extensions are possible. (If you don’t know the service date, locate the proof of service.)
- Preserve evidence now: do not delete emails, texts, voicemails, documents, social media posts, or anything relevant. Save copies (PDFs/screenshots) and back them up.
- Create a single organized folder (digital + one paper copy) labeled by document type and date.

2) Essential questions to ask Maria (use these in your meeting or call)
A. Deadlines & procedure
- What is the exact deadline to file a response or appearance? Are there different deadlines for different types of service?
- What will happen at the preliminary hearing and what should I expect? What outcome could occur there (scheduling, temporary orders, dismissal, settlement discussions)?
- Are any immediate motions we should file (to extend time, for a protective order, for injunctive relief, etc.)?

B. Evidence & discovery
- Do I have enough admissible evidence now to survive the next hearing or to support any emergency relief?
- What specific documents or types of evidence would strengthen my position most?
- Should we issue a preservation letter or litigation hold to the ex-employer, ex-spouse, or third parties (ISP, phone company, bank)?
- Will we need subpoenas for records (bank, phone, employer HR records)? If so, when should we issue them?
- Do we need to take depositions before the hearing, or can we rely on declarations/affidavits?

C. Witnesses & testimony
- Which witnesses should I identify now? Which are likely to be most helpful?
- Should we obtain written affidavits or sworn declarations from witnesses before the hearing?
- Are there experts we should consult or retain (forensic accountant, damages expert, digital forensics)?

D. Authenticity & admissibility
- How do we authenticate electronic evidence (texts, emails, social media)? Do we need metadata or forensic images?
- Will any of my evidence raise hearsay/authenticity issues, and how can we address them?

E. Strategy & risk
- What are the legal strengths and weaknesses of my case as you see them?
- What are realistic outcomes at the preliminary hearing vs. trial?
- What discovery should we prioritize now to preserve key evidence and build the strongest case?

F. Costs & timing
- What will these immediate actions cost (subpoenas, expert reports, depositions)?
- How long will it take to get third-party records once subpoenas are issued?

G. Settlement & alternatives
- Is settlement likely/possible at this stage? If so, should we prepare settlement parameters or a demand letter?
- Would mediation be useful before trial? When should that be scheduled?

H. Practical next steps for me
- What specific documents should I deliver to you before our next meeting?
- How should I format and label documents/exhibits for you and for court?
- Who should I avoid contacting (to prevent spoliation or harmful communications)?

3) Evidence checklist — collect and preserve these items
- Employment contract, offer letters, non-compete/NDAs, severance agreements, amendments
- All communications with the employer and ex-spouse about the dispute: emails, texts (SMS/iMessage), DMs, WhatsApp, Facebook/Instagram messages, LinkedIn messages
- Paystubs, W-2s/1099s, bank statements showing compensation or payments, expense reimbursements
- Performance reviews, disciplinary notices, HR complaints, HR policies, company handbook
- Any signed documents, invoices, receipts, expense records, timecards
- Screenshots + original digital files of relevant social media posts or online statements
- Call logs, voicemails, metadata for digital files if possible (dates, times, sender)
- Witness names, contact details, and short notes on what each can testify to
- Photographs, videos, calendars, location logs relevant to the dispute
- Anything showing damages or mitigation efforts (job searches, new employment offers)
- Prior agreements or communications documenting terms you say were breached

4) How to preserve and present evidence
- Save original digital files; export emails to PDF with headers if possible; take full-page screenshots of texts with visible timestamps.
- Keep a written timeline of events (dates, actions, who said what) and link each timeline entry to the document(s) that support it.
- Number exhibits consistently (Exhibit 1, Exhibit 2) and prepare an index.
- Get written affidavits from key witnesses if depositions aren’t immediately practical.

5) Questions about discovery and subpoenas to ask Maria
- What is our plan and timeline for written discovery (requests for production, interrogatories, requests for admission)?
- When should we notice depositions? Which parties/third-parties should be deposed early?
- Do we need a court order to obtain any records, or can we get them voluntarily?

6) Preparing for the preliminary hearing
- Prepare a short exhibit binder with the most important documents (contract, key messages, financial records).
- Prepare a concise timeline summary you can present verbally or to the judge.
- Decide which witnesses (if any) should testify at the preliminary hearing vs. via affidavit.
- Ask Maria whether she wants a proposed order or proposed schedule for the court.

7) Possible red flags to tell Maria immediately
- Any evidence that might be deleted or altered (texts scheduled to be erased, accounts that could be closed).
- Contact from the opposing party asking you to sign documents or admitting fault.
- Any new witnesses or evidence that appeared after service.

