Short note: I’m not your lawyer. The items below are to help you prepare questions and organize evidence to discuss with Maria.

1) Prioritized questions to ask Maria Santos (short, clear phrasing)
- Procedural / deadlines
  - What is the exact date and purpose of the preliminary hearing? What will the judge decide at that hearing?
  - What are the deadlines I must meet now (deadline to file an answer, motions, discovery responses)?
  - Do we need to ask the court for more time (continuance) or is the schedule manageable?

- Evidence & discovery
  - Based on the claims and defenses, do you think we need additional evidence now, or can we gather it during discovery?
  - Which specific documents or categories of evidence are most critical before the preliminary hearing?
  - Should we issue preservation letters or immediate subpoenas to preserve documents or ESI (email, phones, servers)?
  - Should we consider an early request for production, requests for admissions, or depositions before the preliminary hearing?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses would be most persuasive at the preliminary hearing or trial?
  - Do we need sworn statements or declarations from any witnesses now?
  - Are there witnesses we should depose quickly to lock in testimony?

- Admissibility & authentication
  - Will any of our key documents likely face admissibility or hearsay challenges? If so, how can we authenticate them now?
  - Do we need forensic copies of electronic records (emails, device content)?

- Strategy & risks
  - What is our short-term strategy for the preliminary hearing (seek dismissal, present limited evidence, focus on procedural issues)?
  - What are the main weaknesses in our position and what evidence would address them?
  - Are there counterclaims or defensive steps we should prepare now?

- Experts, valuations, and cost
  - Will we need an expert for damages, employment practices, or contract interpretation? If so, when must an expert report be ready?
  - What are estimated costs and timelines for obtaining necessary evidence (forensics, subpoenas, expert reports)?

- Practical/logistics
  - What should I avoid doing (e.g., contacting the opposing party, posting on social media)?
  - Who on your team do I coordinate with for document collection or questions?

2) Detailed evidence checklist (by category) — prioritize collecting these items now
- Contract and employment documentation
  - Original signed contract(s), amendments, or offer letters
  - Employee handbook, policies, disciplinary records, written warnings
  - Termination notice, severance or settlement offers, emails about termination

- Communications
  - All emails, text messages, DMs, and recorded calls between you and the former employer (including managers, HR) and with your ex-spouse that relate to the contract or dispute
  - Any voicemail transcripts or notes about phone calls
  - Dates, times, and short summaries of relevant conversations

- Financial / damages evidence
  - Pay stubs, payroll records, commission statements, bonus notices
  - Bank records showing payments or withheld amounts
  - Invoices, billing records, profit/loss statements tied to dispute items
  - Evidence of mitigation efforts (job-search logs, applications, interview schedules)

- Personnel & HR
  - Performance reviews, promotion documents, job descriptions
  - HR investigation files, complaint forms, witness interviews
  - Any internal emails about your role or performance

- Witness information
  - Names, roles, contact info for coworkers, supervisors, HR reps, or third parties who observed or handled relevant events
  - Short written statements or timeline from any willing witness (date-stamped)

- Documents proving timeline
  - Calendar entries, meeting invites, timestamps, screenshots
  - Deliverables, project records, code commits, drafts, completed work proofs

- Electronic evidence / ESI
  - Export and back up relevant email folders (native format if possible), chat logs, phone backups
  - Preserve metadata (don’t alter files if possible)
  - Device serial numbers, account names, login dates if relevant

- Copies of pleadings / court documents
  - Proof of service paperwork (who served you, when)
  - Complaint and any attachments from the opposing party
  - Any motions, orders, or notices already filed

3) Immediate actions to take (do these today or as soon as possible)
- Preservation
  - Do NOT delete relevant emails, texts, or files. Preserve devices and accounts.
  - Make backups of email folders and relevant documents (external drive, encrypted cloud).
  - Send a short preservation note to any custodians (HR, colleagues) if your lawyer advises — or ask Maria to send a legal hold immediately.

- Communication
  - Avoid direct contact with the opposing party (your ex-spouse) about the case unless instructed by Maria.
  - Avoid discussing the case on social media or public forums.

- Collect and organize
  - Start a single folder (physical or digital) with subfolders: contracts, communications, financials, HR, witnesses, court docs.
  - Create a simple timeline (dates and one-line descriptions). This will help Maria quickly see the sequence.

- Evidence priority (what to gather first)
  1. Complaint and proof of service + response deadline.
  2. Any contract(s) and termination-related documents.
  3. Emails/texts directly related to the alleged breach or dispute.
  4. Pay/financial records tied to claimed damages.
  5. Names/contact info of likely witnesses and any written witness statements you can obtain quickly.

- If you suspect tampering or deletion
  - Tell Maria immediately. She may request forensic preservation (imaging a device) or seek emergency relief.

4) What to bring to your meeting with Maria
- Printed/organized copies (and a digital copy) of everything above, especially the complaint and any emails or contracts
- A one-page timeline of events (dates, short description)
- List of witnesses with contact details and short notes about what they can testify to
- List of questions from section 1 (so you don’t forget)
- Notes about prior communications with your ex-spouse or employer (dates/times)

5) Questions to ask about evidence-gathering logistics
- Who will handle subpoenas and drafting requests for production?
- Will your office contact HR or IT at the former employer for records, or should I request them personally?
- How should I preserve/transfer electronic files to you securely?
- What level of detail/format do you want for witness statements?

6) Possible next legal steps to discuss with Maria
- File answer or responsive pleading (and strategy for admissions/denials)
- Request for a continuance if more time needed for evidence
- Early discovery requests/subpoenas to preserve critical evidence
- Motions (dismissal, temporary relief, protective order) that may be appropriate before or at the preliminary hearing
- Settlement talks or mediation — are we open to that now?

7) Templates I can prepare for you (tell me which you want)
- Draft email to Maria summarizing questions and asking for a meeting
- Preservation/legal-hold template she can send to custodians (if she requests)
- Short witness statement template you can give coworkers
- A formatted timeline document

