Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Maria Santos. Below is a focused list of questions to bring to your attorney, a prioritized evidence checklist, immediate preservation steps, and a short script/email you can use to ask for a meeting. If you tell me the exact next-court date and what you’ve already been served with (complaint, motion, temporary orders, etc.), I can tailor everything further.

Questions to ask your attorney (grouped by topic)
- Case overview and immediate priorities
  - What is our objective for the upcoming hearing (procedural, temporary relief, timeline to trial)?
  - Based on what I’ve already been served, do we need to file anything before the hearing? (answer/response, motion, request for continuance)
  - What is the deadline to respond to the complaint or motions? What happens if we miss it?
- Evidence sufficiency & gaps
  - Do we currently have enough evidence to meet our burden for the issues likely to be heard at the preliminary hearing?
  - What specific facts or elements are we missing that the judge will want to see at this stage?
  - Which pieces of evidence are likely to be decisive vs. just supporting?
- Documents & records to obtain
  - Which documents should I gather and bring you immediately? (contracts, emails, pay stubs, bank records, text logs, performance reviews, termination letters, severance agreements)
  - Are there third-party records we should subpoena now (employer payroll, bank statements, text/message logs from service providers)?
- Witnesses & statements
  - Which witnesses do we need to contact and have statements from before the hearing?
  - Should we get written affidavits, sworn statements, or signed declarations from witnesses now?
- Digital evidence & authentication
  - Do any electronic communications (emails, texts, Slack/Teams messages) need to be preserved in a particular way to ensure admissibility?
  - Will metadata or chain-of-custody issues be important for any documents or files?
- Discovery and timing
  - What discovery tools should we use immediately (requests for production, interrogatories, depositions, subpoenas)?
  - How long will those discovery steps take — can we realistically obtain key records before the next hearing?
- Motions & tactical options
  - Should we move for a continuance if key evidence can’t be obtained in time?
  - Are there pre-hearing motions we should file (motion to compel, motion for protective order, motion in limine)?
- Experts & valuation
  - Do we need an expert (accountant, damages expert, forensic IT) before the hearing or can that wait until later?
  - If so, how long will it take and what will it cost?
- Admissibility concerns
  - Are there hearsay or authentication issues with any of my documents (voicemails, text messages, screenshots)?
  - What steps should I take so these materials are admissible?
- Cost, timeline, and settlement
  - What are the expected costs to obtain additional evidence (subpoenas, expert reports, depositions)?
  - Is settlement or mediation advisable now, or should we wait until after discovery/trial prep?
- Next steps and responsibilities
  - What do you need from me this week? (documents, witness contacts, authorizations, signed releases)
  - Who will contact third parties for records, and what authorization do you need from me?
  - If I find additional evidence, how and when should I deliver it to you?

Prioritized evidence checklist (what to gather now)
- Core contract documents: original contract, amendments, addenda, signed offer letters, NDAs, severance agreements.
- Communications: all emails, texts, DMs (Slack/Teams), voicemail transcripts, and relevant social media messages between you and the employer/ex-spouse. Include date/time stamps.
- Financial records: invoices, receipts, pay stubs, direct deposit records, bank statements showing disputed payments or transfers.
- Personnel/employment records: performance reviews, disciplinary notices, termination letter, attendance or time records, benefits paperwork.
- Notices and service papers: copy of the complaint, summons, any motions you were served with, proof of service documents.
- Timeline: a one-page chronological timeline of key events (dates, short descriptions, who was involved).
- Witness list: names, contact info, short summary of what each witness can attest to.
- Physical evidence/photos: any photos, physical items, or screenshots (include original files if possible).
- Third-party records: communications or records from vendors, HR, payroll companies, banks.
- Backup copies: native file versions (not just screenshots) when possible — preserve metadata.

Immediate preservation steps (do these now)
- Do not delete any relevant emails, texts, or files. Stop any routine deletion/auto-cleaning.
- Save/export emails and texts as PDFs or native files; take screenshots that show timestamps and sender/recipient info.
- Back up devices (phone/computer) to a secure location (external drive or encrypted cloud).
- Save originals and make multiple copies (one for you, one for your attorney).
- Note who else may have relevant documents and preserve those (ask them to save things).
- Ask your attorney about sending a written “preservation/litigation hold” notice to relevant custodians or your former employer.

How to present materials to your attorney
- Create a short index (single page) listing what you are giving them: document name, date, and one-line why it matters.
- Number exhibits and use consistent filenames (e.g., 01_EmploymentContract.pdf, 02_Email_2024-10-12_from-XYZ.pdf).
- Bring originals plus a folder of labeled copies, and an electronic copy (USB or secure upload).
- Highlight the most important passages and write 1-2 sentence notes explaining their significance.

