Good — I can help you get organized and make the most of your time with Maria Santos. Below is a focused, practical checklist and a set of specific questions (with short scripts you can use) so you can determine whether you need more evidence before the next hearing and what to prioritize now.

Brief notes up front
- I’m not your lawyer; this is to help you prepare questions and a plan to discuss with Maria. She will give the legal advice and make strategic decisions.
- Because you’ve been served and have a short window, act quickly to preserve evidence and confirm deadlines.

Immediate actions (do these right away)
1. Locate and save the summons/complaint and any court notice — note the deadline to respond. Bring copies to your attorney.
2. Preserve everything: do not delete emails, texts, social media posts, voicemails, or documents relating to the dispute. Make backups (export, screenshot with timestamps).
3. Make a chronological timeline of events (dates, times, short notes) — rough is fine; Maria will refine it.
4. Gather all relevant documents now (see Evidence checklist below).
5. Ask Maria immediately whether to file an initial response (answer, motion to dismiss, request extension). If you need more time, ask about requesting a continuance or extension and the likely effect.

Key questions to ask Maria (grouped by topic) — with suggested phrasing
A. Deadlines & immediate procedure
- “What is the exact deadline to file our response, and what are the options if we need more time?” — (Why: ensures you don’t miss default; know extension procedures.)
- “Do you recommend filing an answer, a motion, or asking for more time right away?” — (Why: strategic choice can affect discovery timing.)
- “Is there anything we must do before the preliminary hearing to avoid prejudice?” — (Why: to prioritize tasks.)

B. Evidence collection & preservation
- “What specific documents or records do you need from me before the next date?” — (Why: avoids wasting time gathering irrelevant items.)
- “Should we send a preservation letter to the opposing party or third parties (banks, employers, providers) now?” — (Why: to avoid spoliation.)
- “Do you need original documents or are certified copies/screen captures acceptable?” — (Why: determine format and authentication needs.)

C. Discovery & obtaining evidence from others
- “What discovery tools will we likely use (requests for production, interrogatories, subpoenas) and when should we start them?” — (Why: some records take time to obtain.)
- “Which third-party records should we subpoena immediately (banks, email providers, HR records, phone carriers)?” — (Why: third-party compliance can be slow.)

D. Witnesses & affidavits
- “Who should we identify as key witnesses, and should we get written witness statements/affidavits now?” — (Why: lock in testimony while memories are fresh.)
- “Do any witnesses need subpoenas for the preliminary hearing?” — (Why: ensure attendance.)

E. Forensics & technical evidence
- “Do we need forensics (email headers, metadata, phone forensics)? If so, how quickly can we get that done and at what cost?” — (Why: some evidence requires specialists.)
- “How should I preserve electronic evidence to maintain metadata?” — (Why: improper saving can destroy metadata.)

F. Strategy & settlement
- “Given the facts, do you think a settlement attempt is appropriate before the hearing?” — (Why: saves time/cost if viable.)
- “What evidence will substantially strengthen our negotiating position?” — (Why: focus on high-value items.)

G. Costs & timeline
- “What are estimated costs and expected timeline to get the additional evidence or experts we may need?” — (Why: plan resources and expectations.)
- “If we gather extra evidence, will that justify asking the court for a continuance?” — (Why: weigh benefit vs. delay risk.)

H. Court logistics & hearing prep
- “What should I bring to the preliminary hearing?” — (Why: be prepared with exhibits, copies, witness info.)
- “How long is the hearing likely to be and what will be decided?” — (Why: manage expectations.)

Concrete evidence checklist (collect and organize these)
- The contract(s) in dispute: original, signed copies, amendments, attachments, and any severance/termination letters.
- All communications between you and ex-spouse (emails, texts, DMs, social posts) related to the contract or dispute — preserve originals and export copies with timestamps.
- Phone records/call logs (if relevant) and voicemail exports.
- Financial records: bank statements, invoices, payment records, payroll, receipts, accounting records showing flows related to the contract.
- HR records if employer-related: offer letters, performance reviews, termination notices, benefits paperwork, policy documents.
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, project files, drafts of work product, version history.
- Witness names and contact info and short summaries of what each can testify to.
- Photographs, receipts, physical evidence, and any chain-of-custody notes.
- Any prior agreements, NDAs, IP assignments, or communications referencing the disputed terms.
- Logs of lost income or damages, with supporting documents (tax returns, invoices, estimates).

How to prioritize evidence with a short window
1. Critical evidence to preserve immediately: contract, complaint, communications that support your key claims/defenses, and any documents the opposing party could destroy.
2. Next priority: third-party records that take time (bank, email provider, phone carrier, HR). Ask Maria about subpoenas now.
3. Lower priority: items that are easy to obtain quickly (copies of emails you already have, receipts).

Suggested short scripts you can use in conversation or email to Maria
- Quick email/phone opener:
  “Maria — I’ve been served in the Maricopa County civil case (prelim hearing scheduled for [date]). I’d like to confirm the response deadline and prioritize evidence preservation. Can we meet or talk ASAP? I can bring the complaint, contract, and a timeline I drafted. Also, please advise if we should send preservation letters or issue subpoenas now.”

- To ask for preservation/subpoena:
  “Please advise whether you want me to sign an authorization or whether you will prepare preservation requests/subpoenas now for [bank/email/HR].”

- To request a continuance:
  “If we need more time to obtain third-party records or forensics, do you recommend requesting a continuance? What grounds do we need to show?”

What to bring to your first meeting about evidence
- Copy of the complaint and any service papers.
- The original contract and related documents.
- Chronological timeline of events (even a bullet list).
- Copies/exports of communications (emails, texts).
- List of witnesses with contact info and a short note of what each knows.
- A short statement of what result you want (damages, specific performance, dismissal, etc).
- Questions from the list above you most want answered.

Red flags to mention to Maria right away
- Opposing party has threatened to delete evidence or has made unusual requests for deletion.
- You already lost access to accounts or devices that contain evidence.
- Any urgent deadlines for third-party records (e.g., retention policies that purge after a short time).

Offer to help
- I can convert the suggested scripts into a polished email to Maria Santos, or draft a preservation letter checklist, or create a one-page timeline you can print and bring to the meeting. Would you like me to draft an email to Maria now? If yes, tell me how formal you want it and whether to send it (I can draft or, if you want, prepare it for sending).

