Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Good — below is a focused, practical checklist and a set of specific questions you can take to Maria Santos so you can determine whether you need to gather more evidence before the next date. I’ll also include an immediate PRESERVATION checklist and a short template you can copy/paste to email or message your attorney. If you want, I can draft a tailored message to Maria using details you provide (dates, what you already have, etc.).

Immediate preservation (do these now)
- Don’t delete or alter any emails, text messages, social-media posts, voicemails, or files that relate to the contract, your employment, or communications with the opposing party. 
- Make backups (PDF or screenshot + save originals) of:
  - All emails and attachments (include headers if possible)
  - Text messages and DMs (screenshot with timestamps)
  - Employment records (offer letter, employment contract, job descriptions, policy manuals)
  - Pay stubs, commission statements, bonus notices, pay records
  - Performance reviews, warnings, disciplinary records, termination letters
  - Any severance or settlement offers
  - Calendars/meeting notes that reference events/discussions
  - Invoices, receipts, bank records relevant to claimed damages
- Put a litigation hold notice in writing to any third party who might have relevant records (employer HR, payroll, cloud providers) — ask Maria if she wants you to send it or if she will.
- Preserve original physical documents in a safe place; scan and store copies in at least two secure locations.

Essential questions to ask Maria Santos (grouped)
1) Case status & deadlines
- What exact filings and deadlines are upcoming (preliminary hearing date, hearing purpose, deadlines to respond to complaint, discovery schedule)?
- What is the deadline to file a formal answer or responsive pleading? Can we extend it and what are the pros/cons?
- Are there any imminent court-required disclosures or document exchanges I must complete before the preliminary hearing?

2) Burden of proof & key legal elements
- What specific legal claims/defenses are likely to be central to the preliminary hearing and to trial (breach of contract elements, damages, mitigation, defenses)?
- What evidence do we need to prove each essential element of my claim/defense?

3) What evidence to prioritize now
- Which documents or witness statements do you consider “must have” before the preliminary hearing?
- Which items are “helpful but can wait until discovery/trial”?
- Are there items that could be lost or destroyed if not collected immediately (emails on a third-party server, phone messages, HR records)?

4) Witnesses
- Which witnesses should I identify and get statements from now (co-workers, supervisors, HR, clients, anyone present at key meetings)?
- Should we ask witnesses to provide written affidavits now or wait for depositions?
- How should I preserve witness contact info and availability?

5) Discovery & third-party records
- Should we serve subpoenas now (for payroll records, email logs, bank statements, vendor records)? If yes, to whom?
- What discovery do you plan to request first (documents, interrogatories, admissions, depositions)?
- Are there any protective orders or confidentiality concerns we should address when obtaining employer records?

6) Electronic evidence and authentication
- Do we need metadata, email headers, or logs to authenticate digital files? If so, can you tell me how to export/save them correctly?
- Should I get forensic preservation of phones/computers if I’m worried about tampering?

7) Experts and damages
- Will we likely need an expert (employment damages, forensic accountant, vocational expert)? If so, when must we retain one?
- What financial documentation should I gather now to calculate damages (pay history, tax returns, benefits statements)?

8) Motions/strategy
- Are there strategic motions to consider before the preliminary hearing (motion to dismiss, motion for a more definite statement, motion to compel)?
- Is settlement or mediation an option at this stage? Should we prepare settlement figures and supporting evidence?

9) Logistics & evidence presentation
- What is the format you prefer for receiving documents (PDFs, labeled folders, spreadsheet index)?
- Do you want hard copies or an electronic exhibit binder? Deadline for providing exhibits for the hearing/trial?
- How much time do you need to review materials I gather?

10) Costs and timing
- What are the estimated costs and timeframes for additional discovery (subpoenas, depositions, experts)?
- How do these costs affect our approach (limited discovery now vs. broader gathering)?

Document/evidence checklist to gather (prioritize by Must have / Important / Nice to have)

Must have (collect before the preliminary hearing)
- Complaint, summons, and any court filings already served
- Your responsive pleadings (or draft if not yet filed)
- Employment contract(s), offers, amendments
- Termination letter, discharge notice, severance agreement
- Pay stubs, commission statements, bonus documentation covering dispute period
- Emails and texts directly about the disputed contract terms, performance, pay, or termination
- Any written communications with the ex-spouse related to the contract/dispute
- HR correspondence and internal investigation files (if applicable)
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, and annotated timeline of events

Important (collect as soon as possible)
- Performance reviews, disciplinary records, attendance records
- Internal policies/manuals that affect the contract or duties
- Customer/partner communications relevant to performance or damages
- Payroll and benefits records (for damage calculations)
- Witness contact info and short statements about what they observed

Nice to have (obtain if reasonable)
- Drafts of the contract or negotiation emails showing intent or changes
- Backup of deleted messages if relevant (with attorney guidance)
- Social-media posts or other public statements that support your timeline
- Third-party records (bank statements, vendor invoices) that show financial impact

How to organize what you give your attorney
- Create a single index (spreadsheet) with: Document ID, short description, date, custodial source, and file name.
- Name files consistently: YYYY-MM-DD_source_shortdesc.pdf (e.g., 2024-11-15_email_JSmith_offer.pdf)
- Group documents by topic (contract, pay, communications, HR, damages)
- Provide a one-page timeline of events (dates + one-line description) and a 1–2 paragraph statement of your position/goal
- Flag any items you believe are time-sensitive or at risk of loss

Suggested questions to add if you want a concrete next-step plan
- Given the upcoming hearing, what is our exact evidence-gathering plan and deadline?
- Which documents should I prioritize sending you this week?
- Do you recommend issuing preservation letters/subpoenas now? If so, can you prepare them?
- Should I contact potential witnesses now, and what should I tell them?
- What can I do personally to strengthen our position before discovery starts?

