Good — I can help you get organized. Below are focused, practical questions and a short checklist you can use with Maria Santos to decide whether you need more evidence before the next court date. I’ll group questions so you can quickly run through them at your meeting or call.

First, quick priorities to confirm now
- Confirm the exact next court date and what type of hearing it is (preliminary status/scheduling, motion hearing, etc.). That determines how much factual proof you must have ready.
- Confirm the deadline to file a response/answer and any deadlines for discovery or motions.
- Immediately preserve all potentially relevant materials (see checklist below).

Questions to ask Maria (grouped and with short phrasing you can use)

1) Case status, deadlines, and immediate filings
- “What exactly will happen at the next hearing (status conference, scheduling, temporary relief)?”
- “What are the deadlines for filing my answer, counterclaims, or motions?”
- “Are there imminent deadlines I can’t miss (response to service, initial disclosures, proposed scheduling order)?”
- “Should we ask for an extension if we need more time to gather evidence?”

2) Burden of proof, key elements, and what evidence is necessary
- “What elements of the contract dispute must I prove at this stage?”
- “Which facts are most critical to the court’s decision at the preliminary stage?”
- “Based on what you’ve seen, what specific documents or testimony would most strengthen my position?”

3) Evidence collection and preservation
- “What documents should I preserve immediately (contracts, emails, texts, pay records, performance reviews, termination letters, severance offers)?”
- “Should I place a litigation hold on my devices and accounts? What steps should I take to preserve text messages, deleted emails, or cloud backups?”
- “Are there third-party records we should subpoena now (employer HR files, payroll records, server logs, phone records)?”
- “How should we document chain of custody and authentication for electronic evidence?”

4) Witnesses, declarations, and depositions
- “Which witnesses should we identify now and do we need written declarations for the preliminary hearing?”
- “Should we try to schedule depositions before the hearing?”
- “Do we need expert witnesses? If so, when should we retain them?”

5) Discovery strategy and timing
- “What discovery should we prioritize before the next date (documents, interrogatories, admissions, subpoenas)?”
- “Can discovery be expedited, or do we request an accelerated schedule from the court?”
- “Are there protective orders we should ask for (privacy, business confidentiality)?”

6) Motions and legal strategy
- “Is there any motion we should file now (motion to dismiss, motion for temporary relief, motion to compel, TRO)?”
- “Do you think settlement or mediation is realistic at this stage, or should we push forward with discovery?”
- “What risks or weaknesses do you see in our position and how should we address them with evidence?”

7) Costs, logistics, and client responsibilities
- “What will gathering the evidence cost (subpoenas, expert fees, deposition costs) and how should I budget for it?”
- “What information or actions do you need from me this week to be ready?”
- “Are there confidentiality concerns if I share documents or communications with you/my witnesses?”

8) Communications and conduct
- “Should I avoid contacting the opposing party (my ex-spouse) or their counsel directly?”
- “If contacted by them, how should I respond (or should I refer them to you)?”

Document and evidence checklist — collect/organize these now
High priority (gather before the next hearing if possible)
- Signed contract(s) and any amendments
- Notices, termination letters, severance agreements, or offer letters
- All email chains and text messages related to the dispute (export and save backups/screenshots)
- Pay stubs, invoices, billing records, payroll, bank transfers relevant to the contract
- Performance reviews, disciplinary records, HR emails
- Any NDAs, non-compete, or related employment documents
- Documents showing damages/losses you claim (invoices, accounting)
- Service papers and all court documents you have received
- A concise timeline of key dates (contract dates, breach, termination, communications, service date)

Secondary items (helpful if available)
- Witness names, contact info and short notes on what they can testify to
- Server logs, access logs, version history of files (if relevant)
- Calendar entries, meeting notes, audio/voicemail transcripts (if lawful)
- Screenshots of social media or deleted posts if relevant—note date/time preserved

Preservation steps (do these now)
- Stop deleting related emails, texts, documents, or files.
- Export/backup relevant emails and texts (PDFs or message exports); take dated screenshots.
- Save originals of physical documents and make copies.
- Put a note to yourself to tell potential witnesses not to destroy records.
- Ask Maria about issuing a formal litigation hold to your former employer/third parties if appropriate.

Sample short script you can use with Maria
“Maria — quick checklist before the preliminary hearing: can you confirm what will occur at the hearing and our immediate deadlines? I’m preserving all emails, texts, contracts, payroll, and HR records now. Which of these should I prioritize sending to you, and do you want me to request subpoenas or retain any experts at this point?”

Next steps I can do for you
- Draft a concise email to Maria with the above questions (I can write it in her voice tailored to the facts you give).
- Create a prioritized evidence checklist or timeline template you can fill in.
- Draft sample language for a preservation/litigation hold notice (for your own records) to discuss with Maria.

